INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5613,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12036651','Inborn Errors of Immunity Contribute to the Burden of Skin Disease and Create Opportunities for Improving the Practice of Dermatology.','Colvin A, Youssef S, Noh H, Wright J, Jumonville G, LaRow Brown K, Tatonetti NP, Milner JD, Weng C, Bordone LA, Petukhova L.','2023','underrecognized illuminates underrecognized bastarache alkuraya jurgens alkuraya ceyhan birsoy dellefave groopman claussnitzer petukhova gwass langlais modulations plenge ieis subclassified tangye ieis iei immunologist thalhammer ieis immunodeficiencies 2020a ieis iei butte ieis ieis pazmandi 2020a atschekzei colvin petukhova iei ieis ieis pazmandi atschekzei ieis langlais iei ieis iei iei ieis iei carves ieis annotate iei iei ieis iei ectodysplasin ieis noncutaneous noncancerous iei noncutaneous noncancerous iei iei noncancerous ikbkb lpin2 mefv ncstn psenen pstpip1 tbx1 xiap inversa ikbkb mefv ncstn psen1 psenen pstpip1 aire icos ifng il1rn il2ra card11 dock8 foxn1 ifih1 il6r pgm3 znf341 bach2 c17orf62 card11 card14 casp8 cd247 cd3g cfhr4 cib1 csf2rb cyba cybb diaph1 dnajc21 dock8 foxn1 gins1 ifih1 ikzf2 il17ra il2ra il6r il6st il7r itpkb lig4 msn mthfd1 mysm1 ncf1 ncf2 ncf4 ndnl2 nfkb1 nod2 pepd pgm3 ptprc rbck1 rltpr rorc rtel1 sbds spink5 srp54 stat1 stat5b tbx1 tmc6 tmc8 tnfaip3 tnfrsf11a wipf1 zap70 znf341 ap1s3 card14 il23r il36rn stat2 tnfaip3 traf3ip2 acp5 aire dclre1c lrba nbn nlrp1 plcg2 slx4 iei annotate iei monarch iei noncancerous clouds monarch extracutaneous noncancerous ieis perturb coherency diamonds illuminated ieis ieis catalogs incentivized taxonomies il6r il6r il6r il6r il6r ieis cumulatively ieis taxonomies ieis suppurutiva ieis ieis colvin petukhova iei iei eczematous 2020b ieis hypopigmentation ieis ieis ieis collapsing jaccard ieis ieis ieis ieis curates ieis tangye identifications szklarczyk clouds clouds wordart iei noncancerous monarch mcmurry schriml inputted comma noncutaneous noncancerous 2023q1 iei uhlen identifications reactome wikipathways jaccard medoids rousseeuw iei nondescript rcy3 gustavsen jaccard louvain traag networktoolbox iei nutraceutical nihms2072782 supplementary_tables kl2tr001874 ul1tr001873 k01ar075111 r01ar080796 npt klb r35gm131905 r01lm012895 u01hg008680 69th montagna iei jidonline smedley butte trinity alkuraya atschekzei dubrowinskaja sogkas e002561 bastarache bastarache lyttle bearden khiabanian nondegenerate ceyhan birsoy jayakumaran kemel misyura aypar jairam buckingham jhangiani sobreira networktoolbox claussnitzer dermitzakis hurles colvin petukhova dellefave cirino callis esplin hatchell freitag howson rainbow il6r 358ala e1003444 gysi kellis barabasi groopman marasa petrovski milo rasouly gustavsen isserlin demchak rcy3 f1000res jaccard jaccard jaccard jurgens morrill chaffin pirruccello halford rousseeuw medoids langlais fodil gros mcmurry balhoff borromeo monarch 2020a diathesis stiehm 2020b pazmandi kalinichenko ardy boztug petukhova plenge scolnick altshuler kaganovsky malouf huey tsinajinne schriml schor olley mccracken d1255 markiel ozier baliga ramage szklarczyk gable wyder huerta cepas d607 tangye bousfiha rundles thalhammer kindle nieters seppanen mrj traag waltman eck louvain uhlen fagerberg hallstrom lindskog oksvold mardinoglu feunang marcu d1074 immunodeficiencies nihms2072782 supplementary_tables','Abstract Opportunities to improve the clinical management of skin disease are being created by advances in genomic medicine. Large-scale sequencing increasingly challenges notions about single-gene disorders. It is now apparent that monogenic etiologies make appreciable contributions to the population burden of disease and that they are underrecognized in clinical practice. A genetic diagnosis informs on molecular pathology and may direct targeted treatments and tailored prevention strategies for patients and family members. It also generates knowledge about disease pathogenesis and management that is relevant to patients without rare pathogenic variants. Inborn errors of immunity are a large class of monogenic etiologies that have been well-studied and contribute to the population burden of inflammatory diseases. To further delineate the contributions of inborn errors of immunity to the pathogenesis of skin disease, we performed a set of analyses that identified 316 inborn errors of immunity associated with skin pathologies, including common skin diseases. These data suggest that clinical sequencing is underutilized in dermatology. We next use these data to derive a network that illuminates the molecular relationships of these disorders and suggests an underlying etiological organization to immune-mediated skin disease. Our results motivate the further development of a molecularly derived and data-driven reorganization of clinical diagnoses of skin disease. INTRODUCTION Single-gene disorders contribute to the population burden of disease and are underrecognized in clinical practice. These diseases have traditionally been characterized as causes of rare congenital or early-onset disease and associated with severe symptoms that bear little relevance to most people with a medical condition. However, sequencing studies are increasingly challenging these notions. The phenotypic spectrum of Mendelian diseases is now known to include more subtle phenotypes that can increase diagnostic delays. For example, even though cystic fibrosis is considered a childhood disease, one study found that 8% of patients received their first diagnosis over the age of 30 years ( Bastarache et al., 2019 ). Population-based sequencing studies have found that up to 2% of patients carry rare pathogenic variants in medically actionable disease genes ( Alkuraya, 2021 ; Jurgens et al., 2022 ; Rao et al., 2023 ). These studies investigated rare variants in small sets of genes (n = 25–54). Given that there are >4,000 genes that can cause a single-gene disorder ( Alkuraya, 2021 ), 2% is likely a lower bound on the estimate for the population burden of monogenic etiologies. Estimates are generally between 10 and 30% in cohorts of research participants with well-characterized disease ( 100,000 Genomes Project Pilot Investigators et al., 2021 ; Ceyhan-Birsoy et al., 2022 ; Dellefave-Castillo et al., 2022 ; Groopman et al., 2019 ). These studies clearly support the notion that monogenic etiologies contribute to the genetic architecture of all common diseases ( Claussnitzer et al., 2020 ; Petukhova, 2020 ) and are underdiagnosed in clinical practice. Studies of rare single-gene disorders generate knowledge that is relevant to common, polygenic forms of diseases. For example, risk variants identified in GWASs are located near Mendelian disease genes more often than expected by chance ( Blair et al., 2013 ; Chong et al., 2015 ; Langlais et al., 2017 ). Thus, there are sets of genes for which rare pathogenic variants can cause disease for some individuals and for which genetic variants that are common in the population increase the risk of disease for many individuals by causing more subtle biological modulations. The existence of such genes argues for the presence of an etiological architecture that is shared between rare Mendelian diseases and prevalent multifactorial diseases. This hypothesis is further supported by the discovery that therapeutic targeting of Mendelian disease genes is efficacious for patients who lack rare pathogenic variants ( Chong et al., 2015 ; Plenge et al., 2013 ). We further propose that although fundamental etiological architecture is shared between rare and common diseases, it is much easier to infer from monogenic etiologies. Disease mechanisms are more easily derived from rare pathogenic variants, which implicate individual genes. In contrast, GWAS risk variants tend to be intergenic and can affect multiple transcripts. Therefore, mapping out pathways implicated in a set of single-gene disorders allows us to prioritize a discrete set of genes and pathways for therapeutic targeting and helps to define disease relationships, resolving etiological heterogeneity. Immune-mediated diseases contribute to the burden of skin disease. Monogenic etiologies that cause immune dysregulation are known as inborn errors of immunity (IEIs) and may be subclassified into primary immune regulatory disorders for immune dysregulation, primary immune deficiencies for defects in host defenses, and primary atopic disorders. These single-gene disorders comprise a heterogeneous group of more than 450 different diseases ( Tangye et al., 2022 ). Although IEIs have historically been associated with increased susceptibility to infection, patients may manifest predominantly with other clinical effects of immune dysregulation, including autoimmunity, allergy, pathological inflammation, or cancer. In fact, >20% of patients diagnosed with an IEI by a clinical immunologist do not initially manifest with an infection ( Thalhammer et al., 2021 ). That estimate depends on which specialist is encountering the patient. For example, endocrinologists managing patients with diabetes are more likely to see autoimmune manifestations rather than infectious ones. Nonetheless, this class of disorders has been well-studied and has generated extensive knowledge about disease mechanisms and the basic biology of the immune system. The clinical translation of this knowledge is improving health outcomes and healthcare efficiency for patients and family members. Detailed molecular, cellular, and clinical phenotypes associated with IEIs facilitate diagnoses and are curated by several groups, such as the United States Immunodeficiency Network, the Primary Immune Deficiency Treatment Consortium, the European Society for Immunodeficiencies, and the International Union of Immunological Societies. Genetic testing is available for many of these disorders. Commercially available screening panels provide a cost-effective method for obtaining a diagnosis that provides a description of the molecular pathology and helps to resolve etiological heterogeneity. For example, clinically indistinguishable atopic disorders can arise from perturbations in distinct pathways ( Milner, 2020a ). Knowing which pathway is causing disease for an individual patient before initiating treatment improves healthcare efficiency. The evidence base for the use of targeted treatments for some IEIs is expanding, further increasing the clinical utility of a genetic diagnosis of an IEI ( Abraham and Butte, 2021 ). Finally, genetic testing for IEIs also improves diagnostic yields through cascade testing of family members. The spectrum of clinical features associated with IEIs overlaps with common forms of immune-mediated diseases. For example, inflammatory bowel disease ( Pazmandi et al., 2019 ), atopy ( Milner, 2020a ), psoriasis ( Atschekzei et al., 2022 ), and hidradenitis suppurativa ( Colvin and Petukhova, 2023 ) have all been identified among research participants with an IEI. Diagnostic studies of IEIs among research participants with a diagnosis of a common disease demonstrate high yields from genetic testing. IEIs have been identified in up to 30% of research participants in inflammatory bowel disease cohorts ( Pazmandi et al., 2019 ), lupus cohorts ( Lee et al., 2022 ; Wu et al., 2023 ), and psoriatic arthritis cohorts ( Atschekzei et al., 2022 ). Furthermore, genes that cause IEIs are enriched among risk loci identified in GWAS of common inflammatory diseases, including inflammatory bowel disease, rheumatoid arthritis, and multiple sclerosis, among others ( Langlais et al., 2017 ). This suggests that some patients in these cohorts who lack a rare pathogenic variant have disease that could be mitigated by targeting IEI pathways. The identification of undiagnosed IEIs among dermatology patients facilitates the use of tailored therapies and individualized preventative measures for themselves and their family members. It helps to advance genetic testing in dermatology and promises to improve the clinical management of skin diseases that arise from either an IEI or perturbations of pathways implicated by an IEI. To resolve the causal structure of immune-mediated skin disease, we performed a series of bioinformatic analyses on genes that cause IEIs. We identify a subset of 316 IEI genes that are associated with clinical features in the skin, including 90 that are annotated as causes of common autoimmune or inflammatory skin diseases. We next use these genes to identify enriched pathways and targeted drugs ( Figure 1 ). We use these data to create a network of functionally related genes that suggest clinically meaningful subtypes of skin disease. Thus, our work provides a framework to better understand, identify, and prioritize pathways that are important for disease pathogenesis. This foundation carves out a path to improve the diagnosis, targeted intervention, and prevention of skin diseases. It motivates a greater implementation of genetic testing and deep phenotyping in dermatology and a molecularly derived reorganization of clinical diagnoses. Figure 1. Bioinformatic filtering of genes that cause IEIs. Open in a new tab We used large-scale publicly available data to annotate genes that underlie IEI and identify a subset of 316 genes that are relevant to dermatology patients with either rare or common skin diseases. Of 90 genes that are causes of common skin diseases, 63 significantly enrich a pathway, and 22 implicate a drug target. IEI, inborn errors of immunity. RESULTS Skin-related features are prevalent among IEIs We obtained a list of 451 genes that cause an IEI, which includes 447 protein-coding genes that are known to harbor rare pathogenic variants resulting in clinical manifestations of immune dysregulation ( Supplementary Table S1 ). We mapped 445 of these protein-coding genes to 17,296 terms curated in 15 databases that have been constructed to capture biologically meaningful connections between proteins ( Supplementary Table S2 ). We queried these terms with a set of skin-related features and identified 414 terms that describe pathology in the skin. These terms capture molecular (e.g., ectodysplasin A signaling in hair follicle development, chemokine signaling pathway), cellular (e.g., keratinocyte, melanoma cell line), and clinical (e.g., slow-growing hair, skin rash) features as well as diagnoses (e.g., psoriasis, vitiligo). This way, we identified 316 IEIs that are annotated with skin-related features, suggesting that they are contributing to dermatological disease outcomes. Patients who carry a rare pathogenic variant in one of these genes are at increased risk of cutaneous and noncutaneous diseases. A total of 90 of these genes are associated with a common noncancerous skin disease ( Table 1 ). Although these disorders are individually very rare in the population, patients with an undiagnosed IEI, particularly those who present with a medically complex case of a common skin disorder, represent salient opportunities to improve care with genetic testing. Therefore, we summarize not only cutaneous but also noncutaneous clinical features associated with pathogenic mutations in these 90 genes, which included anemia, arthritis, pneumonia, recurrent infections, and abdominal symptoms such as diarrhea, abdominal pain, and colitis ( Figure 2 ). These 90 genes that are associated with a common noncancerous skin disease warrant focused attention owing to immediate clinical consequences in screening and management for patients harboring a pathogenic mutation. However, all 316 IEI genes with any skin manifestations provide valuable information about the etiological architecture of immune-mediated skin disease. Therefore, we included all 316 genes with cutaneous manifestations in our pathway enrichment analysis. Table 1. IEI Genes Associated with Common Noncancerous Skin Disease Disease Inborn Error of Immunity Genes Acne IKBKB, LPIN2, MEFV, NCSTN, PSENEN, PSTPIP1, TBX1, XIAP Acne inversa, Hidradenitis, Hidradenitis suppurativa IKBKB, MEFV, NCSTN, PSEN1, PSENEN, PSTPIP1 Alopecia areata AIRE, CTLA4, ICOS, IFNG, IL1RN, IL2RA, PMS2 Atopic dermatitis CARD11, DOCK8, FOXN1, IFIH1, IL6R, JAK1, PGM3, ZNF341 Eczema BACH2, C17orf62, C5, CARD11, CARD14, CASP8, CD247, CD28, CD3G, CFHR4, CIB1, CSF2RB, CTLA4, CYBA, CYBB, DIAPH1, DNAJC21, DOCK8, FOXN1, FOXP3, GINS1, IFIH1, IKZF2, IL17RA, IL2RA, IL6R, IL6ST, IL7R, ITPKB, JAK1, LIG4, MSN, MTHFD1, MYSM1, NCF1, NCF2, NCF4, NDNL2, NFKB1, NOD2, PEPD, PGM3, POLE, PTPRC, RBCK1, REL, RLTPR, RORC, RTEL1, SBDS, SPINK5, SRP54, STAT1, STAT3, STAT5B, TBX1, TMC6, TMC8, TNFAIP3, TNFRSF11A, WAS, WIPF1, ZAP70, ZNF341 Psoriasis AP1S3, CARD14, IL23R, IL36RN, STAT2, TNFAIP3, TRAF3IP2 Vitiligo ACP5, AIRE, CXCR4, DCLRE1C, LRBA, NBN, NLRP1, PLCG2, SLX4 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: IEI, inborn errors of immunity; STAT, signal transducer and activator of transcription. We used large-scale publicly available data to annotate genes that underlie IEI and identified a subset of 90 genes annotated with the name of a common skin disease (first column). Disease annotations are derived from The Monarch Initiative and The Disease Ontology, which were both accessed through STRING. These annotations may not be exhaustive of all disease associations reported in the literature. The full set of annotations and their sources are in Supplementary Table S2 . We propose that some patients presenting with these diseases may have an undiagnosed IEI, particularly those with severe and/or recalcitrant symptoms and comorbid conditions consistent with associated clinical features in Figure 2 and Supplementary Table S2 . Figure 2. Clinical features associated with 90 inborn errors of immunity genes that include a common noncancerous skin disease. Open in a new tab Word clouds were created using annotations generated by The Monarch Initiative and The Disease Ontology and accessed through the STRING database. ( a ) Cutaneous features are sized proportionally to their frequency of appearance among gene annotations. ( b ) Extracutaneous features sized proportionally to their frequency of appearance among genes associated with the seven common noncancerous skin diseases studied, weighted by their proportion of each disease’s associated terms. Frequencies of terms are limited by the extent to which they have been studied. For example, eczema has been extensively studied among individuals with single-gene causes of immune dysregulation, whereas monogenic etiologies involving other diseases, such as hidradenitis suppurativa and alopecia areata, have not been as extensively studied. Pathway enrichment analysis provides a biological framework for understanding skin homeostasis Single-gene disorders provide robust biological insight into disease mechanisms and reveal pathways with fundamental roles in maintaining health. To identify the biological processes implicated by IEIs that perturb skin biology, we used these 316 genes to perform pathway enrichment analysis. We identified 282 pathways significantly enriched (false discovery rate < 0.001) by 235 of these genes. These pathways capture a diverse set of biological processes related to immune surveillance and response ( Supplementary Table S3 ). Redundancy in the sets of genes that are enriching multiple pathways suggests that this large group of 282 pathways is converging onto a much smaller set of functional processes. Therefore, we next used hierarchical clustering and identified 19 groups of significantly enriched pathways. Within each of these groups, pathway enrichment is driven by a similar set of genes, and pathway names have semantic coherency ( Supplementary Table S3 ). To understand the molecular relationships among the 235 single-gene disorders implicated by our pathway enrichment analysis, we visualized the network that arises from membership of individual genes in pathway clusters ( Figure 3 ). Within the network, pathway clusters that share more genes are located more proximal to each other. Importantly, genes that are located more proximal to each other are likely to share molecular pathologies. Thus, the physical distance between genes in this network provides a metric for estimating the molecular distance between monogenic etiologies. These data set a foundation for future research aimed at making predictions about the clinical effects of rare pathogenic variants, including drug responses. This visualization also highlights key regulators in skin health and suggests an etiological architecture among skin diseases that arises from immune dysregulation. Figure 3. Molecular relationships among genes that cause an inborn error of immunity associated with clinical manifestations in the skin. Open in a new tab Pathways (blue diamonds) were determined by gene enrichment and an iterative process of hierarchical clustering and semantic synthesis. Genes that enriched represented pathways are indicated by circles. Edges represent pathway membership. Within the network, monogenic etiologies that are located closer together share more molecular annotations. Likewise, because pathways located closer together within the network share more genes, they are likely to be responsive to similar perturbations. The clinical relevance of these relationships remains to be determined. Further information about gene identity and pathway groupings is found in Supplementary Table S3 . Community membership is found in Supplementary Table S1 . Clinical implications of the biological framework illuminated by IEIs in skin disease We used DrugBank to identify drugs that target IEIs with clinical manifestations in the skin ( Wishart et al., 2018 ). DrugBank catalogs Food and Drug Administration–approved drugs and compounds undergoing investigational or experimental drug trials. Because these agents are already in clinical trials or in the market and many of them are already verified for safety, the time required for clinical trials to test new indications can be significantly reduced. DrugBank links agents with gene targets, allowing for the use of genetic evidence to prioritize drug repurposing studies. We identified 127 drugs representing 210 unique gene target—drug associations. Thirty-nine genes were associated with more than one drug. The most highly targeted genes are involved in complement signaling and are targeted by 26 different drugs. In total, only 75 of the 316 genes that we prioritized are represented in DrugBank ( Supplementary Table S4 ). DISCUSSION Limitations in deriving a diagnosis from the clinical presentation of disease have been appreciated for some time. The recognition that people who share a diagnosis can have different molecular causes of disease and that there are important clinical implications to these molecular subtypes has driven precision medicine initiatives and incentivized the development of new disease taxonomies. Studies of monogenic etiologies, enhanced by technological developments improving the efficiency of large-scale sequencing, have led to important discoveries that highlight the clinical utility of a genetic diagnosis. Today, it is recognized that monogenic etiologies are more prevalent in clinical practice than previously appreciated and can underlie disease that is clinically indistinguishable from common polygenic disorders. Studies of single-gene disorders generate robust knowledge about the causes of disease and improve success rates in drug development. For example, studies of a rare loss-of-function variant in IL6R helped to resolve the disease mechanism underlying a GWAS association and demonstrate clinical relevance. A common missense allele in IL6R increases the risk of asthma and atopic dermatitis but is protective against a number of other inflammatory and autoimmune diseases ( Ferreira et al., 2013 ). The mechanism for this was unclear, but studies of the IL6R loss-of-function rare variant showed that it leads to severe atopic dermatitis and abnormal inflammatory skin lesions, confirming that it is the loss of receptor function that contributes to risk. Moreover, it suggests that patients with an IL6R common risk variant might not benefit from therapeutic blockade of IL6R because it will mimic the effect of loss-of-function variants, eliminating its protective effects and causing opposing clinical consequences. This demonstrates that studies of single-gene disorders may provide clinically relevant information for common risk variants that inform both disease pathogenesis and management. In this study, we have integrated knowledge about IEIs to demonstrate that cumulatively, they are contributing to skin disease etiologies. Our work provides a resource for investigating complex dermatology cases, further delineating the contributions of IEIs to common skin diseases, and developing a new data-driven reorganization of skin disease taxonomies. There are several limitations to our work that warrant consideration. Knowledge about individual genes is highly skewed, with most studies focused on a small handful of genes and a substantial number of genes for which very little information is available ( Gates et al., 2021 ). This disparity of knowledge across all genes in the genome creates a bias in the distribution of annotations and creates limitations in interpreting our data. For example, our annotation exercise was unable to find any skin-related annotations for 129 genes. The median number of annotations for these genes (n = 220) is substantially lower than the median for all genes that we analyzed (n = 359). Therefore, we are unable to estimate how many of the genes without any skin annotations truly do not have clinical effects on the skin. In addition to false negatives, false positives may also be present in these data. The databases that we utilized in this study do not offer metrics to directly assess the strength of evidence for their annotations. Clinical validation is a large undertaking and will be the focus of future research. Similarly, distributions of clinical features are influenced by the extent to which they have been studied. Eczema is prevalent among IEIs but has also been extensively studied. Our group has previously provided evidence that hidradenitis suppurutiva is underreported among IEIs, in part because its natural history precludes presentation before adolescence, and most IEIs are diagnosed in childhood ( Colvin and Petukhova, 2023 ). More generally, once a patient receives a diagnosis of an IEI, cutaneous symptoms are less likely to elicit a diagnosis of a common immune-mediated disease. For instance, the IEI autosomal dominant hyper-IgE syndrome caused by dominant negative mutations in STAT3 causes a skin disease characterized as eczematous dermatitis instead of merely being attributed to atopic dermatitis, a common immune-mediated disease ( Milner, 2020b ). Similarly, it is possible that some sets of symptoms that are highly represented among clinical features may be attributed to a missed diagnosis of a common skin disease. For example, although there are nine IEIs annotated as causes of vitiligo, there are an additional 54 genes annotated as causes of hypopigmentation. Therefore, our work provides an opportunity to further delineate single-gene causes of vitiligo. Similarly, further examining the IEIs associated with alopecia may identify additional genetic causes of alopecia areata. Our pathway enrichment analysis delineates a set of immune pathways that are relevant to maintaining skin health and provides a framework for understanding the molecular relationships among IEIs with skin-related features. The majority of IEIs (316 of 451) were found to be annotated with skin features. This limits our ability to identify immune pathways that are unique to the skin or to prioritize pathways that may have stronger effects on the skin than on other organs. In addition, enrichment analyses will fail to identify relevant pathways that have not been well-studied and/or are not well-annotated with genes. There are limitations to visualizing gene–pathway interactions. For example, the size of a network places constraints on effective visualization. We performed data reduction to address this challenge, collapsing pathways with similar gene membership patterns as identified with clustering algorithms. Furthermore, from a technical perspective, the network layout can be influenced by the parameters of the force-directed algorithm. To visualize a network of varying sizes, it is necessary to explore the optimal parameter range. Finally, to avoid a computational burden, we did not construct a fully connected graph to assign edge weights for the force-directed algorithm. Instead, we assigned weights to the top 10% of edges on the basis of their functional similarity, to optimize the graph layout. Functional similarity was determined on the basis of the similarity of node connectivity using Jaccard index (Hancock et al., 2014). We identified 75 genes that are targeted by drugs that are annotated to be approved in DrugBank. DrugBank uses the term approved to mean that it has been approved in at least one jurisdiction, at some point in time. This does not mean that the drug is currently approved or available, just that it has been approved and marketed at some point, somewhere. Different jurisdictions also have different concepts for approval. A second limitation of these data is that we have not specified the genetic mechanisms for the IEIs. There are some genes in this list that are known to cause disease by both loss-of-function and gain-of-function mechanisms. Furthermore, we have not specified mechanisms of action for the drugs that we list in Supplementary Table S4 . Therefore, this information has to be interpreted with caution. It is meant only as a starting point for further research and is not intended to inform medical decisions. Although our work implicates 316 IEIs in skin disease, only 75 of these are annotated in DrugBank, suggesting many unmet needs among these disorders. Importantly, this finding also suggests that there may be clinical utility in further developing our framework for defining molecular relationships among these IEIs. Identifying sets of IEIs driven by perturbations in similar pathways could facilitate drug repurposing studies. Despite these limitations, our work provides a foundation for advancing precision medicine in dermatology. It supports a greater implementation of genetic testing and deep phenotyping in dermatology. It also delineates a set of pathways that are important for disease pathogenesis and tissue homeostasis. Finally, it motivates the further development of a molecularly derived and data-driven reorganization of clinical diagnoses of skin disease. MATERIALS AND METHODS Gene annotations The International Union of Immunological Societies curates information about IEIs. We converted gene names provided by the International Union of Immunological Societies ( Tangye et al., 2022 ) to official gene symbols and Entrez identifications for downstream processing ( Supplementary Table S1 ). To systematically identify genes associated with clinical manifestations in the skin, we leveraged an online database that integrates several publicly available sources of information about proteins, including clinical and molecular features: STRING-db ( Szklarczyk et al., 2019 ). We manually curated STRING-generated functional annotations using a set of 24 search terms related to skin and skin disease ( Supplementary Table S2 ). Word clouds Word clouds were generated using an online word cloud generator at https://wordart.com/create . We focused on the 90 genes that cause an IEI that is associated with a common noncancerous skin disease ( Table 1 ). Clinical features and their frequencies of association with genes were obtained by extracting annotations from two databases identified with STRING. Monarch is a genetic analysis tool that integrates gene, genotype, variant, disease, and phenotype data ( McMurry et al., 2016 ). Diseases Ontology provides descriptions of human disease terms, phenotype characteristics, and related medical vocabularies ( Schriml et al., 2022 ). We limited terms to those with one or two words. Terms were inputted in CSV (comma-separated value) format without stemming. All cutaneous terms were included in the figure; noncutaneous terms were limited to those associated with at least four of the seven common noncancerous skin diseases studied. Frequencies of terms are limited by the extent to which they have been studied. For example, eczema has been extensively studied among individuals with single-gene causes of immune dysregulation, whereas monogenic etiologies involving other diseases, such as hidradenitis suppurativa and alopecia areata, have not been as extensively studied. Pathway enrichment analysis Pathway enrichment analysis was performed with the DAVID (Database for Annotation, Visualization, and Integrated Discovery), version 2023q1 ( Supplementary Table S2 ). We imported 316 IEI genes with skin annotations. We specified the background gene set with 14,161 genes determined to be expressed in the skin by The Human Protein Atlas ( Uhlén et al., 2015 ). Gene names were imported as Entrez identifications and then converted to official gene symbols. We queried categories of annotations related to biological processes and pathways, including Gene Ontology biological processes, Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes pathways, Reactome pathways, and Wikipathways. We set a significance threshold of false discovery rate < 0.001. Network construction Sets of significantly enriched pathways (false discovery rate < 0.001) were implicated by similar sets of genes. To reduce this redundancy, therefore, we computed the Jaccard distance (Hancock et al., 2014) between pathways on the basis of the common genes shared by them and utilized Partitioning Around Medoids ( Ferreira et al., 2013 ) clustering ( Kaufman and Rousseeuw, 1990 ). This identified groups of significantly enriched pathways that converge on a singular underlying biological process. Through an iterative process, we identified 19 pathway clusters that provided the best distinction between biological processes ( Supplementary Table S3 ). Pathway clusters and gene names were imported into Cytoscape, version 3.10.0 ( Shannon et al., 2003 ), to construct a network visualizing molecular relationships among genes that cause an IEI with skin symptoms. This accounted for 1,105 relationships between 221 genes and 19 pathway groups, each representing a unique relationship ( Supplementary Table S3 ). Genes that did not enrich a pathway or that only enriched a nondescript pathway (e.g., R-HSA-1643685-Disease, R-HSA-168256-Immune System) could not be represented in the network. Next, we utilized a force-directed algorithm in the RCy3 package ( Gustavsen et al., 2019 ) for Cytoscape’s layout. In particular, to effectively map the pathway and gene nodes on the basis of their functional similarity, we incorporated weights in the clustering of nodes. This was accomplished by considering the additional connectivity between the top 10% gene–gene and pathway–pathway edges, as determined by the Jaccard similarity index, while implementing the force-directed algorithm. Furthermore, we applied Louvain method ( Traag et al., 2019 ) using the NetworkToolbox package ( Christensen, 2018 ) in R for identifying network communities. This provided an optimal graph layout that facilitated the visualization and analysis of the pathway–gene interactions. Furthermore, community detection allowed us to assign individual genes to one of six detected communities representing six distinct functional categories ( Supplementary Table S1 ). Drug target identification and analysis We used DrugBank, version 5.1.10, data to identify drugs that target the 316 IEI genes implicated in skin phenotypes. We mapped genes to gene products, gene products to drug targets, and finally drug targets to relevant drugs. We include only those drugs reasonably available at this time, defined as those that are listed as approved or nutraceutical according to DrugBank and not withdrawn. We include Anatomical Therapeutic Chemical data for each drug pulled from DrugBank. Supplementary Material Supplementary Tables Supplementary Table S1. Gene Names Used in Analyses and Results of Filtering and Analyses Inborn Errors of Immunity Genes that cause an inborn error of immunity (column A) were annotated in STRING to identify a subset that are associated with clinical features in the skin (column G). This subset of genes was next used for a pathway enrichment analysis in DAVID, which identified 282 pathways significantly enriched by 235 of these genes (column J). Data-driven analyses of pathway membership reveal molecular relationships of these genes and suggest the presence of six mechanistic communities (column L). Drugs that target individual genes were identified in the DrugBank database (column M). Supplementary Table S2. Results of Functional Annotations Performed in STRING STRING Annotation Results Annotations derived from STRING for inborn error of immunity genes that are associated with clinical features in the skin are shown. All annotations for this set of genes are included. Filters can be used to select only annotations relevant to the skin (column F), specific skin annotations (column G), or those that implicate a common skin disease (column H). Supplementary Table S3. Results of Pathway Enrichment Analysis and Pathway Clustering DAVID Annotation Results Abbreviation: FDR, false discovery rate. Pathways significantly enriched by inborn errors of immunity that manifest in the skin are shown. Pathway analysis was performed in DAVID. Our threshold for statistical significance is FDR < 0.001. Hierarchical clustering of pathways based on gene membership (column G) was used to identify 30 clusters (column L). Each cluster represents a biologically cohesive set of genes. Cluster descriptions (column M) were manually extracted from enriched terms (column B) and used in our network construction ( Figure 3 ). Supplementary Table S4. Drug Target Identification DrugBank Annotation Results A subset of inborn errors of immunity with skin features that are drug targets is shown. Data are obtained from DrugBank, which was accessed on June 16, 2023. NIHMS2072782-supplement-Supplementary_Tables.xlsx (7.1MB, xlsx) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS LP receives funding and support from the National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (KL2TR001874 and UL1TR001873); the National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases (K01AR075111 and R01AR080796) and the Columbia University’s Precision Medicine Resource, Precision Medicine Initiative, the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center, and the Data Science Institute. NPT and KLB are both supported by the United States National Institutes of Health National Institute of General Medical Sciences (R35GM131905). CW receives funding from R01LM012895 and U01HG008680. This study was completed in New York, NY. This work was presented at the 69th annual Montagna Symposium on the Biology of Skin, “Microbes, Autoimmunity & Cancer,” held October 20–24, 2022. Abbreviation: IEI inborn error of immunity Footnotes CONFLICT OF INTEREST The authors state no conflict of interest. SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIALS Supplementary material is linked to the online version of the paper at www.jidonline.org , and at https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jid.2023.08.018 . Data availability statement Datasets related to this article can be found in supplementary tables . REFERENCES 100,000 Genomes Project Pilot Investigators, Smedley D, Smith KR, Martin A, Thomas EA, McDonagh EM, et al. 100,000 genomes pilot on rare-disease diagnosis in health care—preliminary report. N Engl J Med 2021;385:1868–80. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Abraham RS, Butte MJ. The ne