e to medical students. Acad Emerg Med 2004;11:e6–10. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Canadian Institutes for Health Research Institute of Gender and Health, Johnson J What a Difference Sex and Gender Make: A Gender, Sex and Health Research Casebook. Ottawa, Canada: Institute of Gender and Health of the Canadian Institutes for Health Research and Canadian Women’s Health Network, 2012. [ Google Scholar ] 29. Gender Largo-Janssen T. and sex: issues in medical education. GMS Z Med Ausbild 2010;27:Doc27. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Epstein RM. Assessment in medical education. N Engl J Med 2007;356:387–96. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Association of Academic Women’s Health Programs. Directory of Residency and Fellowship Programs in Women’s Health, 2014. J Womens Health (Larchmt) 2014;23:440–80. [ Google Scholar ] 32. Shumway JM, Hardin RM. AMEE Guide No. 25: The assessment of learning outcomes for the competent and reflective physician. Med Teach 2003;25:569–84. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplemental Data Supplement S1. Sex- and gender-specific education resources. NIHMS1007758-supplement-Supplemental.docx (15.7KB, docx)',3,'2025-10-15 11:25:38','2025-10-15 11:25:38');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5629,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10845037','Healthy Lifestyle and Cognition in Older Adults With Common Neuropathologies of Dementia.','Dhana K, Agarwal P, James BD, Leurgans SE, Rajan KB, Aggarwal NT, Barnes LL, Bennett DA, Schneider JA.','2024','noncurrent pointer residences ffq ffq ffq ffq checkers crosswords puzzles gardening yard calisthenics bicycle riding ffqs healthiest innogenetics bielschowsky lavaan carriership ffq noncurrent noncurrent etables ffq ereferences e235491 e235491 lourida hannon littlejohns licher karamujic comic dhana e374 e383 dhana dhana kwab033 huntley sommerlad dhana e068390 mangialasche ngandu kivipelto multidomain jpad hampel s41380 tangney slows tangney biracial kwg290 hoganson s1355617705050459 mcphillips pellettera wingard criqui biracial stampfer rimm a009849 dietaryguidelines dietary_guidelines_for_americans_2020 sabia fayosse dumurgier whitehall k2927 k2927 aws234 e2259 e2268 rosseel lavaan v048 i02 dhana e811 e822 tangney ffq ereferences e235491 e235491','This cohort study examines data from Rush Memory and Aging Project, including autopsy results and up to 24 years of follow-up, to determine the association between lifestyle and cognitive function independently of Alzheimer disease pathology burden. Key Points Question What is the impact of lifestyle interventions on cognition in older adults as a preventive measure? Findings In this cohort study, a higher lifestyle score (ie, healthier lifestyle) was associated with better cognitive function proximate to death independently of Alzheimer disease pathology burden; that is, neither the strength nor the significance of the lifestyle-cognition association changed substantially when β-amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangle, or other dementia-related brain pathologies were included in the regression model. Meaning A healthy lifestyle may provide a cognitive reserve to maintain cognitive abilities independently of common neuropathologies of dementia. Abstract Importance A healthy lifestyle is associated with better cognitive functioning in older adults, but whether this association is independent of the accumulation of dementia-related pathologies in the brain is uncertain. Objective To determine the role of postmortem brain pathology, including β-amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangles, cerebrovascular pathology, and other brain pathologies, in the association between lifestyle and cognition proximate to death. Design, Setting, and Participants This cohort study used data from the Rush Memory and Aging Project, a longitudinal clinical-pathologic study with autopsy data from 1997 to 2022 and up to 24 years of follow-up. Participants included 754 deceased individuals with data on lifestyle factors, cognitive testing proximate to death, and a complete neuropathologic evaluation at the time of these analyses. Data were analyzed from January 2023 to June 2023. Exposures A healthy lifestyle score was developed based on self-reported factors, including noncurrent smoking, at least 150 minutes of physical activity per week, limiting alcohol consumption, a Mediterranean-DASH Intervention for Neurodegenerative Delay (MIND) diet score higher than 7.5, and a late-life cognitive activity score higher than 3.2. The lifestyle score ranges from 0 to 5, with higher scores reflecting a healthier lifestyle. Main Outcomes and Measures The global cognitive score was derived from a battery of nineteen standardized tests. Brain pathology measures included β-amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangles, global Alzheimer disease pathology, vascular brain pathologies, Lewy body, hippocampal sclerosis, and TAR DNA-binding protein 43. Results Of 586 included decedents, 415 (70.8%) were female, 171 (29.2%) were male, and the mean (SD) age at death was 90.9 (6.0) years. Higher lifestyle score was associated with better global cognitive functioning proximate to death. In the multivariable-adjusted model, a 1-point increase in lifestyle score was associated with 0.216 (SE = 0.036, P < .001) units higher in global cognitive scores. Neither the strength nor the significance of the association changed substantially when common dementia-related brain pathologies were included in the multivariable-adjusted models. The β estimate after controlling for the β-amyloid load was 0.191 (SE = 0.035; P < .001). A higher lifestyle score was associated with lower β-amyloid load in the brain (β = −0.120; SE = 0.041; P = .003), and 11.6% of the lifestyle-cognition association was estimated through β-amyloid load. Conclusions and Relevance This study found that in older adults, a healthy lifestyle may provide a cognitive reserve to maintain cognitive abilities independently of common neuropathologies of dementia. Introduction Primary prevention of Alzheimer dementia through lifestyle modifications has gained much interest from the public and research community in recent years. 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 It is estimated that 40% of worldwide dementia could be theoretically prevented if the population shifts to a healthy lifestyle. 6 We previously reported that lifestyle factors may play a role in dementia prevention in that a healthy lifestyle is associated with a longer life expectancy while living a larger proportion of the remaining years without Alzheimer disease. 7 Overall, the evidence from population-based studies is promising, and a series of clinical trials worldwide, including the US Study to Protect Brain Health Through Lifestyle Intervention to Reduce Risk (US POINTER), are evaluating the efficacy of a lifestyle intervention in preventing cognitive decline among middle-aged and older individuals. 8 However, as individuals age, there is a progressive accumulation of dementia-related brain pathologies (eg, β-amyloid) occurring years before the onset of dementia. 9 This accumulation raises important questions regarding the potential effect of lifestyle interventions for cognition in older adults as preventive measures. We conducted a study among participants of the Rush Memory and Aging Project (MAP) who had died and had an autopsy to evaluate whether lifestyle factors (based on a composite score of healthy behaviors for dementia prevention, including diet, participation in physical and cognitive activities, no smoking, and limiting alcohol intake) are associated with cognition proximate to death independent of dementia-related postmortem brain pathologies, including β-amyloid load and phosphorylated tau tangles. We also estimated the role of vascular disease burden (eg, infarcts, cerebral atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis) in the association between lifestyle score and cognition. Lastly, we evaluated the association of lifestyle with cognition accounting for all dementia-related brain pathologies, including global Alzheimer disease pathology, vascular disease burden, and additionally Lewy body, hippocampal sclerosis, and TAR DNA-binding protein 43 (TDP-43). Methods The Rush MAP is an ongoing longitudinal clinical-pathologic study of aging and dementia comprising older adults living in retirement communities, senior housing, and individual residences in the Chicago area. The MAP study design and methodology have been provided elsewhere. 10 In brief, MAP was initiated in 1997 with an annual assessment of risk factors and blood donation and a detailed clinical evaluation. In addition, participants signed an informed consent for annual assessments and an Anatomical Gift Act document for brain donation at the time of death. MAP was approved by an institutional review board of Rush University Medical Center. In 2004, a nutrition substudy was started, and a self-administered food frequency questionnaire (FFQ) was provided to study participants to collect dietary data, which continued every year afterward. 11 Reporting follows the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology ( STROBE ) reporting guideline. For the current study, we included 586 decedents with valid dietary and lifestyle data, cognitive testing proximate to death, and complete autopsy data at the time of these analyses (eFigure in Supplement 1 ). We compared the demographic (ie, age, sex, and education) and genetic (ie, APOE e4) characteristics of MAP eligible for this study (ie, completed FFQ, but no neuropathology data available) with participants included in the study; the groups did not differ significantly (eTable 1 in Supplement 1 ). Assessment of Lifestyle, Demographic, Genetic, and Clinical Risk Factors Dietary intake was assessed by a 144-item FFQ, a modified version of the Harvard FFQ validated for use in Chicago community residents. 12 Study participants reported the usual frequency of consumption of food items over the previous 12 months. To assess the overall diet quality, we calculated the Mediterranean-DASH Diet Intervention for Neurodegenerative Delay (MIND) diet score without alcohol intake, as described previously. 11 Late-life cognitive activities were assessed using a structured questionnaire that measures participation in 7 cognitively stimulating activities during the past year, including reading, visiting a museum, and playing games like cards, checkers, crosswords, or puzzles. 13 Physical activity was assessed by a validated questionnaire from the 1985 US Health Interview Survey that was adapted for use in older adults. 14 Participants reported the time spent in moderate or vigorous activities, including walking for exercise, gardening or yard work, calisthenics or general exercise, bicycle riding, and swimming. Information on smoking status was reported at the interview at baseline, in which participants specified whether they were current, former, or never smokers. 15 The mean consumption of alcohol was obtained through the FFQs. 12 Lifestyle factors were assessed annually, except for smoking history, which was assessed only at the baseline. We computed means of MIND diet score, cognitive activities, physical activity, and alcohol intake from all available follow-up visits to limit measurement error and misclassification due to self-reporting. 16 Assessment of demographic, genetic, and clinical risk factors is described in the eMethods in Supplement 1 . Lifestyle Score The response to the lifestyle factors was categorized as low risk or “healthy” if a participant was in the healthiest 40% of the participants studied, which corresponds to a MIND diet score (without alcohol) higher than 7.5 and late-life cognitive activities score higher than 3.20. 3 In addition, a healthy lifestyle score included not currently smoking, performing moderate or vigorous exercise activities for at least 150 minutes a week, and light to moderate alcohol consumption (up to 15 g/d for women and up to 30 g/d for men). 17 We included light to moderate alcohol use based on dietary guideline recommendations for individuals in the United States 17 and data focused on alcohol and dementia 18 ; however, according to the dietary guidelines, people who do not drink should not be encouraged to start alcohol consumption. For each lifestyle factor, as described earlier, 3 , 4 , 7 participants scored 1 if they met the threshold for low risk or healthy and 0 if they did not meet the criteria. The 5 individual scores were then summed to obtain a final score in the range 0 to 5, with higher scores reflecting a healthier lifestyle. Assessment of Brain Pathology Postmortem, the brain was removed and sectioned according to a standardized protocol to preserve tissue and quantify Alzheimer disease pathology. 19 β-Amyloid was assessed at multiple brain regions using immunohistochemistry. A video image of amyloid-β–stained sections was used to quantify the β-amyloid deposition in each cortical area. 20 Then we computed the mean percent area and combined it to create a composite continuous summary measure of the total β-amyloid load in the brain. 21 Phosphorylated tau tangle density was quantified as a mean tangle density per mm 2 in each specific brain region using the antibody specific for phosphorylated tau (AT8, 1:1000; Innogenetics). We computed a composite summary measure of phosphorylated tau tangles by averaging the values across specified brain regions. 19 , 21 Bielschowsky silver stain was used to visualize neuritic plaques, diffuse plaques, and neurofibrillary tangles in 5 cortical regions (frontal, temporal, parietal, entorhinal cortex, and hippocampus). The number of neuritic, diffuse plaques, and neurofibrillary tangles identified in a 1-mm 2 area of the highest density in 5 cortical regions was counted and used to develop a composite measure of global Alzheimer disease pathology. 19 , 22 Assessment of atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, microinfarcts, macroinfarcts, and cerebral amyloid angiopathy, Lewy body disease, hippocampal sclerosis, and TDP-43 is described in the eMethods in Supplement 1 . Neuropsychological Testing Cognitive function was evaluated annually with 19 tests, and each of these cognitive test raw scores were standardized (centered and scaled) to the mean and standard deviation at baseline. 23 Then, a mean of standardized cognitive scores for the 19 tests was used to create a composite z score for the global cognitive function. Positive z scores indicate better cognitive performance, whereas negative z scores indicate poorer cognitive performance relative to the mean performance of MAP participants at baseline. For this study, we used global cognitive scores before death. The median time between the last cognitive assessment and death was 0.8 years. Clinical diagnosis of Alzheimer dementia is described in the eMethods in Supplement 1 . Statistical Analysis Characteristics of the study participants are summarized as mean (SD), median (IQR), or numbers and percentages of participants. Linear regression models were used to investigate the association between lifestyle score (continuous per 1-point increase), common brain pathologies, and cognitive functioning proximate to death. Models were adjusted for age at death, sex, education, APOE e4, and time intervals from the last assessment to death (in years) and death to autopsy (in hours). Common brain pathologies such as β-amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangles, and global Alzheimer disease pathology were square root–transformed for better statistical properties. 24 We conducted a series of analyses to determine whether the association between lifestyle factors and cognition is independent of common brain pathologies. First, we estimated the association between lifestyle score and cognition in models with and without common brain pathologies (ie, β-amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangles, or global Alzheimer disease pathology) to determine the change in the magnitude (β estimate) and significance ( P value) of the lifestyle-cognition association when additionally adjusted by common brain pathologies. Second, we evaluated the interaction terms between the lifestyle score and common brain pathologies (ie, β-amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangles, or global Alzheimer disease pathology) and visualized 25 associations on different levels of brain pathology. Third, we estimated the association between the lifestyle score and β-amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangles, or global Alzheimer disease pathology in multivariable-adjusted models. Fourth, we used structural equation modeling with the lavaan R package 26 to estimate the direct and indirect effect of the lifestyle score on global cognition proximate to death, considering brain pathologies (eg, β-amyloid load) as a mediator. In addition, we evaluated the role of cerebrovascular disease pathologies by developing a regression model adjusted by atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, microinfarcts, macroinfarcts, and cerebral amyloid angiopathy. We also estimated the association of lifestyle with cognition while accounting (ie, adjusting) for all dementia-related brain pathologies, including global Alzheimer disease pathology, vascular disease burden, Lewy body, hippocampal sclerosis, and TDP-43. Recognizing that dementia-related brain pathologies may differ by age at death or APOE e4 allele, we performed additional analyses in which we stratified by mean age at death and APOE e4 carriership. We also adjusted our models by body mass index, depressive symptoms, and heart disease because of the effect these risk factors may have in adhering to a healthy lifestyle and cognition. To further examine whether the level of cognition at baseline or before death accounts for the association of lifestyle with cognition, we repeated our sensitivity analysis in individuals without mild cognitive impairment or dementia at baseline (ie, first FFQ assessment) and in those without clinical Alzheimer dementia proximate to death in separate regression models. All the analyses were performed with R software, CRAN version 4.3, and its packages (R Foundation for Statistical Computing). 27 Results The demographic and clinical characteristics of the study sample are shown in Table 1 . Of 586 included decedents, 415 (70.8%) were female, 171 (29.2%) were male, and the mean (SD) age at death was 90.9 (6.0) years. Study participants completed a mean of 15 years of schooling and had a mean (SD) healthy lifestyle score of 2.7 (1.2). Table 1. Demographic, Clinical, and Postmortem Characteristics of the Study Sample. Characteristic Overall, No. (%) No. of participants 586 Demographic Age at death, mean (SD), y 90.9 (6.0) Sex Female 415 (70.8) Male 171 (29.2) Education, mean (SD), y 14.8 (2.8) Genetics APOE e4 carrier 125 (21.3) Clinical a Depressive symptoms, mean (SD) b 1.7 (2.1) Body mass index, mean (SD) c 25.7 (5.0) Heart disease 117 (20.0) Lifestyle factors MIND diet, score mean (SD) d , e 7.1 (1.3) Late-life cognitive activity, mean (SD) d 3.0 (0.6) Physical activity, median (IQR), min/wk d 125.3 (60.2-214.0) Noncurrent smoking f 571 (97.4) Alcohol intake, median (IQR), g/d d 0.3 (0.0-2.9) Lifestyle score, mean (SD) g 2.7 (1.2) AD-related brain pathology Global AD pathology burden, mean (SD) h 0.8 (0.4) β-Amyloid load, mean (SD) h 1.7 (1.2) Phosphorylated tau tangle, mean (SD) h 2.3 (1.3) Arteriolosclerosis, moderate-severe 165 (28.3) Cerebral atherosclerosis, moderate-severe 138 (23.6) Macroinfarcts present 219 (37.4) Microinfarcts present 188 (32.1) Cerebral amyloid angiopathy, moderate-severe 205 (35.0) Lewy body disease 137 (24.0) TDP-43 204 (34.9) Hippocampal sclerosis 55 (9.4) Time to postmortem autopsy, median (IQR), h 7.1 (5.9-9.2) Time from last cognitive assessment to death, median (IQR), y 0.8 (0.4-1.3) Open in a new tab Abbreviations: AD, Alzheimer disease; MIND, Mediterranean-DASH Intervention for Neurodegenerative Delay; TDP-43, TAR DNA-binding protein 43. a Clinical variables were assessed at the last study visit. b Depressive symptoms were assessed with a modified 10-item version of the Center for Epidemiologic Studies Depression scale. Scores range from 0 to 10, with high scores indicating greater depressive symptoms. c Calculated as weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared. d For MIND diet score, cognitive activities, physical activity, and alcohol intake, we computed means from all available follow-up visits. e The MIND diet included 14 items. f Smoking was assessed only at the baseline, and noncurrent smoking included never (n = 348, 59.3%) and former smokers (n = 223, 38.1%). g Lifestyle score had a range of 0 to 5 with higher scores reflecting a healthier lifestyle. h Global AD pathology burden, β-amyloid load, and phosphorylated tau tangle were square root–transformed. Table 2 shows the association of the healthy lifestyle score with global cognition proximate to death before and after adjusting for brain pathology. In the multivariable-adjusted model, a 1-point increase in the healthy lifestyle score was associated with better cognitive performance proximate to death, respectively, with 0.216 standardized units higher (β = 0.216; SE = 0.036; P < .001). After additional adjustment for the β-amyloid load, the healthy lifestyle score remained independently associated with cognition (β = 0.191; SE = 0.035; P < .001). Similarly, the lifestyle score was independently associated with cognition in models adjusted by phosphorylated tau tangle (β = 0.196; SE = 0.031; P < .001) or global Alzheimer disease pathology (β = 0.193; SE = 0.034; P < .001). Table 2. Association of a Healthy Lifestyle With Global Cognitive Functions Proximate to Death (Model A) and After Adjusting for Alzheimer Disease Pathology (Models B to D). Model a β Estimate b SE P value A 0.216 0.036 <.001 B c 0.191 0.035 <.001 C d 0.196 0.031 <.001 D e 0.193 0.034 <.001 Open in a new tab a Models A to D were separate linear regression analyses evaluating the association of lifestyle with cognition and adjusted for age at death, sex, education, APOE e4, and time intervals from the last assessment to death (in years) and from death to autopsy (in hours). b β Estimates are for a 1-unit (ie, 1-point) increase in lifestyle score. c Model B was additionally adjusted for β-amyloid load, which was square root–transformed. d Model C was additionally adjusted for phosphorylated tau tangle, which was square root–transformed. One individual did not have data on phosphorylated tau tangle, so this model included 585 individuals. e Model D was additionally adjusted for global Alzheimer disease pathology, which was square root–transformed. Figure 1 shows the association of lifestyle score with global cognition at different levels of β-amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangle, and global Alzheimer disease pathology. There was no interaction between lifestyle score and brain pathologies ( P > .30 for interactions). The strength (ie, slope) of the association between lifestyle score and cognition was similar across different levels of β-amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangle, and global Alzheimer disease pathology. Figure 1. Interaction Between Common Alzheimer Disease (AD) Brain Pathologies and Lifestyle Score in Association With Global Cognitive Function Proximate to Death. Open in a new tab β-Amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangle, and global AD pathology were square root–transformed. Models A, B, and C included an interaction term between lifestyle score and brain pathology (eg, β-amyloid load) and were adjusted for age at death, sex, education, APOE e4, and time intervals from the last assessment to death (in years) and from death to autopsy (in hours). Shading indicates SD. P > .30 for interactions for each of the studied pathologies. One individual did not have data on phosphorylated tau tangle, so the statistical model with phosphorylated tau tangle (B) included 585 individuals. Table 3 shows the association of the healthy lifestyle score with Alzheimer disease pathology measures. Lifestyle score was significantly associated with the β-amyloid; for a 1-point increase in healthy lifestyle score, there were 0.120 units less β-amyloid load in the brain (β = −0.120; SE = 0.041; P = .003). The β estimates for phosphorylated tau tangle and global Alzheimer disease pathology were β = −0.044 (SE = 0.046; P = .33) and β = −0.022 (SE = 0.013; P = .10), respectively. Table 3. Association of Lifestyle Score With Alzheimer Disease Pathology Measures a . Measure β Estimate SE P value β-Amyloid load −0.120 0.041 .003 Phosphorylated tau tangle −0.044 0.046 .33 Global Alzheimer disease pathology −0.022 0.013 .10 Open in a new tab a Models were adjusted by age at death, sex, education, APOE e4, and time intervals from the last assessment to death (in years) and from death to autopsy (in hours). β-Amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangle, and global AD pathology were square root–transformed. One individual did not have data on phosphorylated tau tangle, so the statistical model with phosphorylated tau tangle included 585 individuals. Figure 2 shows the direct and indirect associations of a healthy lifestyle on global cognition proximate to death. We considered an indirect association of lifestyle on cognition through the pathway of β-amyloid load because of the significant association between lifestyle score and β-amyloid load and β-amyloid load with cognition. The overall estimated association of lifestyle score on cognition was 0.216, with an indirect association through a β-amyloid load of 0.025 (SE = 0.009; P = .008), or 11.6%. That is, 11.6% of the association between lifestyle score and cognition proximate to death in our model was through β-amyloid load. Figure 2. Pathway Analysis Using Structural Equation Modeling to Determine the Direct and Indirect Associations of Lifestyle Score With Global Cognition. Open in a new tab Models A, B, and C were adjusted by age at death, sex, education, APOE e4, and time intervals from the last assessment to death (in years) and from death to autopsy (in hours). β-Amyloid load was square root–transformed. Of the association between lifestyle score and global cognition proximate to death, 11.6% (indirect ÷ total = 0.025 ÷ 0.216) was through the β-amyloid pathway. The direct effect was 88.4% (direct ÷ total = 0.191 ÷ 0.216). The role of postmortem vascular burden and common dementia-related brain pathologies in the association between lifestyle score and cognition proximate to death are shown in eTables 2 and 3 in Supplement 1 . When the lifestyle score and measures of vascular disease pathology were included in the same multivariable-adjusted model, the healthy lifestyle score remained independently associated with cognition, and estimates did not change (β = 0.216; SE = 0.036; P < .001). The lifestyle score was also significantly associated with cognition in the model adjusting for common dementia-related brain pathologies (β = 0.166; SE = 0.033; P < .001). Similar associations were observed when we stratified analysis by age at death (eTable 4 in Supplement 1 ) or APOE e4 allele (eTable 5 in Supplement 1 ); albeit, in individuals who died at 91 years or older, the association between lifestyle and cognition was stronger (β = 0.184; SE = 0.044; P < .001) in a multivariable model adjusted for common dementia-related pathologies. Further adjustment for body mass index, depressive symptoms, and heart disease slightly attenuated the lifestyle-cognition association (ie, β of 0.216 vs 0.208), and these associations were independent of the combined burden of brain pathologies (eTable 6 in Supplement 1 ). Excluding people with poor cognition at baseline or clinical Alzheimer dementia proximate death attenuated the estimates, but the association of lifestyle score with cognition remained statistically significant and independent of common dementia-related brain pathologies (eTable 7 in Supplement 1 ). Discussion In this study of data for 586 well-characterized community-dwelling older adults who had an autopsy, a higher lifestyle score (ie, healthier lifestyle) was associated with better cognitive function proximate to death. The lifestyle-cognition association was independent of Alzheimer disease pathology burden; that is, neither the strength nor the significance of the lifestyle-cognition association changed substantially when β-amyloid load, phosphorylated tau tangle, or other dementia-related brain pathologies were included in the regression model. The independence from Alzheimer disease pathology suggests lifestyle factors may provide cognitive reserve in maintaining cognitive abilities in older adults. Numerous epidemiological studies have shown the potential cognitive benefits of a healthy lifestyle in older adults. 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 7 The focus of the present study was to evaluate whether a healthy lifestyle would be associated with cognition proximate to death in the presence of brain pathology among older adults. 9 We reported that the association of lifestyle with cognition is independent of brain pathology, though a part, estimated to be 12%, was through β-amyloid. While we must use caution in interpreting our findings, in part due to its cross-sectional design, these results support the role of lifestyle in providing cognitive reserve to maintain cognitive function in older adults despite the accumulation of common dementia-related brain pathologies. Further research, such as a life-course study, should investigate the role of duration and change in lifestyle during the study period and estimate whether the change (eg, shift to a better lifestyle) is associated with cognition independently of pathology. It is suggested that lifestyle lowers the risk of Alzheimer disease by reducing the vascular disease burden. 28 While we support this hypothesis (eg, a healthier lifestyle could lead to less diabetes and hypertension, which could in turn lead to a lower risk for dementia), in this autopsy study, we reported that lifestyle score was associated with cognition independently of vascular disease pathology with no change on the estimates. These findings align with our previous work showing that diet and physical activity are associated with cognition independently of vascular disease burden. 24 , 29 , 30 The lack of vascular impact in the lifestyle-cognition association could be attributed to the characteristics of our study sample; for example, age at death was a mean 90 years, and at that age, most people should have developed vascular pathology in the brain, 28 reducing the heterogeneity of the sample and consequently the power of statistical analysis to distinguish significant differences by the lifestyle score. The mechanistic link between lifestyle and cognition could be attributed in part to the antioxidant and anti-inflammatory capacities of each lifestyle factor (eg, nutrition and physical activity) and cognitive reserve (eg, cognitive activities) that contribute to less inflammation and oxidative stress. 31 Further studies, especially research investigating the association of lifestyle factors with markers for inflammation (eg, astrogliosis and microglial activation) in the brain, are necessary to understand the mechanisms of how lifestyle is associated with better cognitive scores proximate to death. Strengths and Limitations Study strengths include the prospective design with a yearly assessment of dietary information and other lifestyle factors (except smoking). The neuropathologic evaluations were performed by examiners blinded to clinical data. Another essential strength is a large autopsy study sample based on enrollment of dementia-free older adults at baseline who were then followed up until death. The limitations of this study should also be noted. The reliance on self-reported lifestyle factors is an important limitation of the study because cognitive impairment could interfere with inaccurate reporting. In addition, cognitive abilities may affect the adherence to lifestyle factors, such as physical activity, introducing bias from reverse causation. We minimized the role of these biases in our analysis by computing the means of each lifestyle factor from enrollment to study until death to account, in part, for the misclassification of a single self-report and reverse causation. Furthermore, we showed that the level of cognition at baseline or before death did not account for the association of lifestyle with cognition. Another significant limitation is that the study sample comprised predominantly White volunteers who agreed to annual evaluations and postmortem organ donation, thus limiting the generalizability of findings to individuals of other races and ethnicities. Our results and conclusions are based on an observational study, and we cannot assume causality of reported associations. Conclusions This study found that a healthier lifestyle was associated with better cognitive function proximate to death independently of common neuropathologies of dementia, suggesting that lifestyle factors may provide cognitive reserve to maintain cognitive abilities in older adults. Supplement 1. eMethods eFigure. Flowchart of study participants eTable 1. Demographic and genetic characteristics of MAP participants eligible for this study (i.e., completed FFQ, but no neuropathology data) with those included in the study eTable 2. Association of a healthy lifestyle with global cognitive functions proximate to death in a multivariable model with and without postmortem vascular pathology eTable 3. Association of a healthy lifestyle with global cognitive functions proximate to death in a multivariable model with and without common brain pathologies postmortem eTable 4. Association of a healthy lifestyle with global cognitive functions proximate to death in a multivariable model with and without common brain pathologies postmortem and on different age strata eTable 5. Association of a healthy lifestyle with global cognitive functions proximate to death in a multivariable model with and without common brain pathologies postmortem and stratified by APOE e4 allele status eTable 6. Association of a healthy lifestyle with global cognitive functions proximate to death in a multivariable model with and without common brain pathologies postmortem and additionally adjusted by BMI, heart disease, and depressive symptoms eTable 7. Association of a healthy lifestyle with global cognitive functions proximate to death in a multivariable model with and without common brain pathologies postmortem in people without cognitive impairment and Alzheimer’s dementia proximate to death eReferences jamaneurol-e235491-s001.pdf (455.4KB, pdf) Supplement 2. Data sharing statement jamaneurol-e235491-s002.pdf (15.4KB, pdf) References 1. Lourida I, Hannon E, Littlejohns TJ, et al. Association of lifestyle and genetic risk with incidence of dementia. JAMA. 2019;322(5):430-437. doi: 10.1001/jama.2019.9879 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Licher S, Ahmad S, Karamujić-Čomić H, et al. Genetic predisposition, modifiable-risk-factor profile and long-term dementia risk in the general population. Nat Med. 2019;25(9)