INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5642,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11541391','From bytes to bedside: a systematic review on the use and readiness of artificial intelligence in the neonatal and pediatric intensive care unit.','Schouten JS, Kalden MACM, van Twist E, Reiss IKM, Gommers DAMPJ, van Genderen ME, Taal HR.','2024','prototyping prototyping limitedly prototyping prognostics prototyping heterogenic prognostics crd42023456299 28th probast trl aeronautics prototyping trl probast trls probast probast probast 28th trls prototyping cries decent probast limitedly trls oncoming landing eases degrading retrain prototyping adegboro probast adegboro trls trls probast probast prototyping 54kb file3 maarten mvg sande genderen huiskens gommers bommel bytes aiindex wornow thapa pfeffer shaky thirunavukarasu dsj elangovan churpek detsky saqib iftikhar neha karishma mumtaz butte cheatham debronkart drazen manrai omenn perlin ramoni sapiro vayena kohane alsentzer balicer bottinger brendel chandak chernew duclos healey arango hundert kowatsch lekadir nigrin ohana shuaib simoncelli alm thaldar tunik wilbanks jcl butte doshi e428 e432 denniston denniston sande oosterhoff bommel gommers genderen muralidharan burgart daneshjou glanville hrobjartsson lalu loder schouten kalden macm imk gommers dampj genderen taal display_record recordid fleischmann reichert cassini markwart trondle savova kissoon schlattmann allegranzi eckmanns mccracken fortenberry hebbar fleuren klausch tlt zwager schoonmade roggeveen swart girbes arj thoral ercole hoogendoorn elbers pwg hond aah imj calster steyerberg boussard kgm westwood probast w33 mankins artemisinnovation trl_white_paper_2004 fleuren thoral shillan ercole elbers pwg prout hajian tilaki caspian strumke thambawita hammou riegler parasa karthikesalingam suleyman steyerberg gerds gonen obuchowski pencina kattan hond aah leeuwenberg hooft imj nijman swj hja aardoom debray schuit smeden steyerberg chavannes kgm calster mclernon smeden wynants steyerberg macaskill mclernon kgm steyerberg calster smeden calster elkin gonen calster steyerberg progn kors rijnbeek amann blasimme vayena madai ghassemi oakden e745 e750 jcc scy kvedar nijman zoodsma koomen sande genderen huiskens veen rer unen gommers bommel e100495 bajwa munir nori e188 e194 cber cdrh regulations_en pauling keck baumbach e16866 adegboro asan dhiman andaur hooft logullo calster smeden probast e048008 54kb file3','Abstract Purpose Despite its promise to enhance patient outcomes and support clinical decision making, clinical use of artificial intelligence (AI) models at the bedside remains limited. Translation of advancements in AI research into tangible clinical benefits is necessary to improve neonatal and pediatric care for critically ill patients. This systematic review seeks to assess the maturity of AI models in neonatal and pediatric intensive care unit (NICU and PICU) treatment, and their risk of bias and objectives. Methods We conducted a systematic search in Medline ALL, Embase, Web of Science Core Collection, Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials, and Google Scholar. Studies using AI models during NICU or PICU stay were eligible for inclusion. Study design, objective, dataset size, level of validation, risk of bias, and technological readiness of the models were extracted. Results Out of the 1257 identified studies 262 were included. The majority of studies was conducted in the NICU (66%) and most had a high risk of bias (77%). An insufficient sample size was the main cause for this high risk of bias. No studies were identified that integrated an AI model in routine clinical practice and the majority of the studies remained in the prototyping and model development phase. Conclusion The majority of AI models remain within the testing and prototyping phase and have a high risk of bias. Bridging the gap between designing and clinical implementation of AI models is needed to warrant safe and trustworthy AI models. Specific guidelines and approaches can help improve clinical outcome with usage of AI. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1007/s00134-024-07629-8. Keywords: Artificial intelligence, Machine learning, Neonatal intensive care unit, Pediatric intensive care unit, Technology readiness level Take-home message This review shows that clinical decision support tools based on artificial intelligence are developed for many different applications in the neonatal and pediatric intensive care unit, but only very limitedly progress beyond the prototyping and development phase. To achieve clinical impact, researchers should consider specific guidelines to develop high quality models with the potential for clinical impact and show clinical usefulness, for example by including calibration as a performance metric, to provide the evidence required for the investments needed to achieve clinical implementation. Open in a new tab Introduction Driven by the potential to enhance patient outcomes and support clinical decisions, artificial intelligence (AI) is set to significantly transform medicine, demonstrated by a rapid increase in research studies, particularly in the field of intensive care medicine [ 1 , 2 ]. This growth is reflected in the rising volume of healthcare research on AI topics [ 2 ]. In addition, the recent developments in the field of generative AI, and more specifically Large Language Models (LLMs), have triggered an increased interest of usage of AI applications in healthcare [ 3 , 4 ]. Despite the challenges in research due to ethical and practical considerations, AI could potentially assist clinicians in diagnostics, prognostics, and treatment across both neonatal, pediatric, and adult critical care to enhance patient outcomes [ 5 – 9 ]. However, translation of the advancements in AI research into tangible clinical benefits and their application at the patient''s bedside is still limited. Currently, less than 2% of AI models reach beyond the prototyping phase and AI models have a high risk of bias [ 1 ]. Importantly, these studies predominantly involve adult patients and the translation to neonatal and pediatric patients remains unknown. This is important, since neonatal and pediatric healthcare is diverse, covering various developmental stages from infancy to adolescence, resulting in a highly heterogenic patient population with significant physiologic and anatomic variations. Differences in disease incidence, presentation, outcomes, and prognosis are substantial between adults and children, which leads to distinct clinical needs, assessments, and treatment approaches in both populations. To bridge the gap between traditional quantitative metrics and implementation of AI at the bedside, several guidelines, such as SPIRIT-AI and CONSORT-AI, have proposed frameworks for safe design, conduct, reporting, and early-stage evaluation of AI studies [ 10 – 12 ]. Given the physiologic and anatomic differences between adults and children, as well as the heterogeneity in developmental stages within the pediatric population, the ACCEPT-AI framework was proposed to develop safe and trustworthy AI applications specifically for children [ 13 ]. This framework underscores the necessity for pediatric-specific AI research and outlines the research and technological considerations essential for the development of safe and trustworthy AI models in this population. Because AI could potentially assist healthcare professionals in diagnostics, prognostics, and treatment across both neonatal and pediatric care, and enhance patient outcomes, it is crucial to determine how “ready” AI technology is for real-world use in critically ill neonatal and pediatric patients. Having this information will enable us to identify potential barriers and move toward clinical deployment. In this systematic review we therefore seek to assess the available literature regarding the current use and the risk of bias of AI in the neonatal intensive care units (NICUs) and pediatric intensive care units (PICUs). In addition, we determined the readiness for clinical implementation of the various AI studies. Methods This manuscript adhered to the 2020 Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-analysis (PRISMA) guideline and was registered in the online PROSPERO database (Record ID: CRD42023456299) prior to initiation of the literature search [ 14 , 15 ]. Inclusion criteria and study identification Publications qualified for inclusion, if they involved patients in the NICU or PICU, assessed AI or Machine Learning (ML) algorithms—defined as computational models capable of learning from extensive data input—and used data that were collected during NICU or PICU stays. To be considered, studies needed to be original research, aimed at directly having impact on clinical care, and available in English full text. Publications were excluded if model development was not the primary study objective, meaning that models were not intended to be implemented into clinical practice, or if algorithms were not applicable during NICU or PICU stay. To prevent exclusion of possibly relevant AI models for infants and children due to varying admission criteria across hospitals worldwide, no age limit was specified. Electronic searches of multiple scientific databases including Medline ALL, Embase, Web of Science Core Collection, Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials, and Google Scholar were performed from inception to March 28th 2024. Electronic search strategies were formulated together with our local librarian. The following terms were used as index terms or free-text words: ‘artificial intelligence’, ‘machine learning’, ‘large language model’, ‘neonatal intensive care unit’, and ‘pediatric intensive care unit’. The full-search strategy is available in the electronic supplementary material (ESM) 1. Study selection Following the study identification process, duplicates were identified and removed using Endnote 20 (Clarivate Analytics, Philadelphia, PA, USA). Two authors (JS and MK) independently reviewed all titles and abstracts to determine whether the inclusion criteria were met. Any disagreements were resolved through consensus. Subsequently, full-text publications underwent independent assessment by these two authors. In the case of conflicting opinions, a third author (RT) had the decisive vote. Reasons for exclusion were documented for each article. Data collection Two authors (JS and MK) independently collected information from all selected studies. Information extracted on study characteristics included: 1) the clinical unit where the model was intended for use (NICU, PICU, or both); 2) study aim, categorized as: mortality prediction, length of stay (LOS)/discharge prediction, disease prediction (with the exception of sepsis), sepsis prediction, treatment effect prediction, ventilation related prediction, disease detection, electroencephalography (EEG) classification, and monitoring. Prediction of sepsis was divided into a separate category since it is a major cause of mortality in both NICU and PICU, and research in adults has shown promising results for detection of early symptoms [ 16 – 18 ]. If the study objective did not meet any of these prespecified categories, it was categorized as other. If a study had multiple aims, all were reported; 3) dataset size, detailing the total number of patients used for data analysis and, if reported, the number of patients with the outcome of interest; 4) level of validation, categorized into internal validation [models are validated on patients who are included in studies’ own dataset], external validation [models are validated on data of patients from a different period in time or from different geographic locations], prospective observational validation, and clinical validation [ 19 ]. Determination of risk of bias Risk of bias was assessed using the PROBAST method (Prediction model Risk Of Bias ASsessment Tool) [ 20 ]. Risk of bias was evaluated across four domains (participants, predictors, outcome, and analysis) and categorized as high, low, or unclear. Results were presented individually for each domain and reported as percentages. No quantitative synthesis was conducted. We did not assess applicability of the developed AI-tools since no specific therapeutic question was defined for this systematic review. Technological readiness To assess the readiness of the studied AI applications for clinical implementation, the Technology Readiness Level (TRL) was obtained. We used a general concept introduced by National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA), which was adapted to the ICU environment. The consecutive levels increase from development to the clinical implementation of AI: problem identification (level 1), proposal of solution (level 2), model prototyping and development (level 3 and 4), model validation (level 5), real-time testing (level 6), workflow integration (level 7), clinical testing (level 8), and integration into clinical practice (level 9) [ 21 , 22 ]. Elaborate TRL definitions are provided in ESM 2. Data analysis Study objectives were ordered on prevalence and displayed in bar plots containing the applicable departments. Full dataset sizes were categorized and depicted graphically for each level of validation. Overall PROBAST scores were displayed including the scores for each specific domain. The TRLs were visually portrayed against the number of studies, aligned with their corresponding level of readiness and year of publication. Numeric outcomes were reported in counts and percentages. Results Study identification The search provided 2287 abstracts of which 628 were identified via Medline ALL, 925 via Embase, 611 via Web of Science Core Collection, 23 via Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials, and 100 via Google Scholar. After title and abstract screening, 455 full-text papers were reviewed for eligibility, of which 262 met the full inclusion criteria. The main reasons for exclusion were that no AI model was developed (55/21%), that the developed model offered no clinical impact (46/18%), or that the used data was not collected during NICU or PICU stay (31/12%), see Fig. 1 . Despite the inclusion of terms related to generative AI and LLMs in the search strategy, no studies that described such models and met the inclusion criteria were found. A list of all included studies and all corresponding collected study items are presented in ESM 3 and supplementary Table 1. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab PRISMA 2020 Flow diagram of the study identification and selection process [ 14 ] Study design and objective Out of the 262 included studies, 173 (66%) were conducted in the NICU and 81 (31%) in the PICU. Only eight studies (3%) developed or evaluated an AI model with the intention to be used in both departments. The most frequent study objectives were predicting diseases other than sepsis (50/19%), predicting mortality (39/15%), and predicting sepsis (39/15%). Forty-four studies (17%) had an objective that was not prespecified. A complete overview of all study objectives is shown in Fig. 2 . Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Number of studies categorized on study objective and intended departments Two hundred fifty five studies (97%) developed AI models using internal validation, and 33 studies (13%) used either temporal or geographic external validation. The median sample size for development studies with internal validation was 230 patients (interquartile range [IQR]: 46–1348). The median sample size for external validation studies was 188.5 (IQR: 31–959). For both levels of validation, the sample size predominantly was less than 100 patients [internal validation: 99 studies (39%), external validation: 13 studies (39%)]. Seventeen internally validated studies (7%) used data on more than 10.000 patients which was only achieved by one externally validated study (3%). Not all studies reported the number of included patients with the outcome of interest. For the 218 internally validated studies that did, the median number of included patients with the outcome was 58.5 (IQR: 25.25–208.75) and for the 26 externally validated studies the median was 35 (IQR: 19.5–267.25). An overview of the different sample sizes per level of validation is provided in Fig. 3 . Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Percentage of studies according to the level of model validation and the total number of patients included in the development or validation process Bias assessment The PROBAST scores were assessed for 259 of the 262 included studies (99%). The results for all four domains as well as the overall score are shown in Fig. 4 . In total, 77% of assessed studies exhibited a high risk of bias. The analysis domain emerged as the primary source of bias with 76% of studies scoring high risk in at least one item of this domain. The outcome and predictors domain had limited impact on the overall scores with low risk of bias in 95% and 90% of studies, respectively. The participants domain provided limited risk of bias with 9% of studies scoring high risk, although 16% of studies were unclear in at least one item of this domain. The complete PROBAST results for each study can be found in supplementary Table 2. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Percentage of risk of bias according to the different PROBAST domains including the overall scores [ 44 ] Technological readiness Figure 5 shows the progression of the levels of readiness of all included studies over time. Two-hundred-and-twenty-nine studies (87%) reached a level ≤ 4 on the ‘level of readiness’ scale, while 31 studies (12%) reached level 5. Only one study collected clinical data prospectively to test an AI model in real-time (level 6), and one study tested the application of in AI model in clinical practice (level 8). We observed an increase in external model validation efforts in the past few years. Especially in 2024, despite the low number of included studies due to the latest publication date of March 28th, the fraction of total studies reporting external validation results of the AI model increased; 5 out of the 14 included studies published this year (36%) against 7 out of 52 studies in 2023 (14%) and 7 out of 42 studies in 2022 (17%). Despite an increase in the number of publications each year, the advancement of TRLs remained limited. Fig. 5. Open in a new tab Number of studies published according to their level of readiness and year of publication Discussion The main finding of this systematic review is that, while the desired movement is to transition toward AI model use at the bedside, the majority of AI models do not progress beyond the development and prototyping phase. Currently, only one study reached this diagonal movement toward clinical use and implementation. Of all studies, 66% regarded an AI model that was intended to be used in the NICU exclusively, over twice the number of studies conducted in the PICU. Due to high heterogeneity in patient age, diagnosis and comorbidities, developing AI models in PICU setting may provide more challenges compared to the NICU, which is a possible cause of the discrepancy in number of published studies [ 23 ]. Forty-four studies had an objective that did not fit into any of the prespecified categories. The majority of these studies were conducted in the NICU. The primary contributors were studies that did not include routinely collected data, but used a new modality; video or sound was recorded to train an AI model. Notably, all such studies were conducted within NICU settings, which is most likely attributable to the prevalence of video cameras enabling parental observation of their newborn child. Multiple researchers recognized the opportunity to use this readily available data to be used for detection of e.g., stress, cries, and emotional states. Out of the 262 included studies, the vast majority assessed performance of the developed AI models using discriminatory metrics, typically the area under the Receiver Operating Characteristics (ROC) curve, and sensitivity and specificity. These metrics have been used in medicine for years to evaluate performance of diagnostic tests and therefore provide easy interpretation by clinicians. Other advantages include that the diagnostic accuracy of AUC is not affected by one specified threshold and that it is independent of disease prevalence [ 24 , 25 ]. However, proper evaluation of model performance for clinical usability requires more information that is generally not provided. One measure that is often overlooked is precision—or Positive Predictive Value (PPV)—which is the proportion of positive cases giving positive results [ 26 ]. In addition, it is recommended to incorporate model calibration into the model performance evaluation. Calibration refers to the agreement between predicted and observed number of events [ 27 , 28 ]. Satisfactory calibration is essential for clinical usefulness of AI models with the aim to support clinical decision making. Models with poor calibration will systematically over- or underestimate a patient’s true risk of a certain outcome, which can be unacceptable in specific cases. Models with high AUC but poor calibration may therefore be less clinically useful than similar models with lower AUC but decent calibration [ 19 , 29 , 30 ]. Despite the additional value of calibration metrics to determine model performance, only 8 (3%) of all included studies incorporated calibration in the performance evaluation. Finally, model performance can be altered by manually fine-tuning decision thresholds. Choosing a correct decision threshold is context dependent and should be performed using a net-benefit analysis. This method incorporates decision curve analysis and contextual risk assessment to decide on the optimal decision threshold [ 31 , 32 ]. More emphasis on the decided decision thresholds is required to deliver clinically usable AI models. The PROBAST scores revealed that the majority of studies had a high risk of bias. The lack of proper study quality was mainly caused by an insufficient sample size relative to the number of included predictive features. Studies often neglected to provide information on the patient selection process, resulting in low scores in the participants domain. Positively, items within the predictors and outcome domains contributed very limitedly to the overall risks of bias. The predominantly low TRLs indicate that the maturation of AI models toward clinical implementation is very limited. Studies incorporating external validation of AI models (level 5) did see an increase in frequency since the first included study that incorporated external validation in 2017. Unfortunately, even after external validation, models are far from ready to be implemented into clinical practice since they are still only fed static retrospective datasets. Only two studies were able to feed real-time data into their AI model. To accommodate the implementation and real-time application of AI models, there are many additional aspects researchers must consider. First, to ensure both the safe development and high quality of AI models with potential for clinical implementation, researchers should prioritize the early integration of specific guidelines, such as the pediatric-specific ACCEPT-AI framework, into their research process [ 13 ]. After the development of AI models in good quality research, real-time deployment requires dedicated information technology and data infrastructure. Most patient data management systems are not designed to be compatible with AI purposes and therefore a novel infrastructure is often required to connect AI models with high quality source data. Source data can originate from multiple monitoring and treatment devices, and needs to be synchronized in time, making such infrastructure highly complex. Also, real-time data may differ from the retrospective dataset used during model development. AI models’ performance depends on the quality of input data which makes validation of this input data highly important. Preprocessing of raw data should ensure that model input data is validated and artifacts are handled correctly. In addition, model output generally should be made visually available since explainability of AI model output is considered essential, especially in healthcare. Insight into model behavior, e.g., why does the model give an alarm for oncoming sepsis, enables health care professionals in interpreting the model output with regard to the clinical status. Explainability of model output is also necessary to assure ethical values, to lead to trustworthy AI, and to ease implementation [ 33 , 34 ]. However, one could also argue that focus on explainability during development may reduce performance [ 35 ]. ‘Research grade’ AI models are generally not designed to be fully user-friendly applications and require programming skills to use and interpret. Developing a model into a usable application for end users (e.g., healthcare providers, parents) requires additional software which adds time and complexity to the entire process of implementing AI models at the bedside [ 28 , 36 , 37 ]. However, the importance of this step should not be underestimated since explainability and visual representation of model output will aid in the end users’ trust in AI, which is key for the adoption of AI in clinical practice. Importantly, stakeholders, such as healthcare professionals and parents, should be addressed and involved throughout every stage of AI model development, from problem identification to clinical implementation [ 38 ]. Since integration of explainable and visual representation of AI model outputs adds complexity, hospitals should invest in a local system that functions as a general landing zone to which multiple newly developed models can be added quickly. Such a centralized system could prevent each researcher having to develop a new user-friendly application which eases the transition from individual models to usable applications. The integration of explainable and visual representation of AI model outputs adds complexity due to several factors. Technically, it involves developing user-friendly dashboards that can effectively visualize complex data in a coherent and easily interpretable format, which requires real-time updates and seamless integration with existing clinical systems. Interpretability is another challenge, as clinicians need to understand how AI models reach their conclusions to trust and act on the recommendations, requiring explanations that are both accurate and clinically meaningful. In addition, there are regulatory and ethical considerations, as the transparency of AI systems is crucial for regulatory compliance and ethical medical practice. A centralized platform would not only streamline the integration of new models, reducing deployment time and resources, but also provide a consistent user experience, minimizing the need for clinicians to adapt to different systems. This centralized system would facilitate the monitoring and maintenance of all AI models, ensuring consistent updates, quality control, and the reliability of interpretability crucial for clinical decision-making. To tackle these challenges a step-by-step approach that improves quality, safety and transparency of AI research was recently proposed [ 38 ]. Lastly, after deployment, continuous maintenance, surveillance and addition of novel data streams remain necessary [ 37 , 39 ]. Models should be evaluated continuously to detect potential drops in performance or signs of bias. Dynamic clinical practice might cause degrading model performance over time due to factors such as changing protocols or new treatment options. Post marketing surveillance, as described by the United States Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the European Medical Device Regulation (MDR) to routinely retrain and assess improvement possibilities is necessary [ 40 , 41 ]. All these additional aspects, beyond initial model development, are time and (financial) resource demanding and require strong evidence for positive economic impact before investments will be made [ 42 ]. This process generally goes far beyond the responsibilities and capabilities of researchers alone and likely is one of the barriers causing the lack of maturation of the described models post development and prototyping. The last systematic review on the use of AI and ML in the NICU and PICU was published in 2022 by Adegboro et al. who had the objective to describe the use of AI to improve any health outcome in NICUs and PICUs [ 43 ]. The authors concluded that further prospective research, incorporating standardized metrics and validated outcome measures, is required to assess the impact of AI. In contrast, the current review performed a more comprehensive literature search and included additional years of inclusion prior to 2010 and between 2021 and 2024. Furthermore, this review incorporated the level of validation and employed the PROBAST method to evaluate the quality of included studies. While Adegboro et al. also reported TRLs, it seems that these were systematically overestimated; the authors observed that most AI models were still in the development stage, yet paradoxically, the reported TRLs predominantly fell between level seven and nine, indicating imminent clinical implementation. The extended literature search and additional outcome measures render this current systematic review a valuable augmentation to the previous work. Some limitations must be acknowledged. First, only studies that developed an AI model using data acquired in the NICU or PICU, intended to be used within these departments and have a direct impact during the admission period were included. To ensure AI models were applicable in these departments, we decided to only include studies in which all data, including outcome data, was collected during a stay in one of these departments. Consequently, AI models predicting long-term outcomes were excluded. Specific AI models that predict long-term outcomes that could actually influence decision making during NICU or PICU stay, such as neurologic development, could have been missed. Secondly, we assessed the risk of bias using the PROBAST method. This tool was developed to assess the risk of bias in prediction models, but it was not developed specifically for AI models. Therefore certain important factors that could contribute to bias may have been overlooked. A dedicated PROBAST-AI tool is currently under development but was unavailable at the time of conducting this review [ 44 ]. Lastly, although comprehensive, the literature search may have missed studies that should have been included. To ensure no clinically implemented AI models were missed, we searched the online open access database for models that have been approved by the FDA. We did not find any additional AI models. Conclusion AI is a promising technology to leverage the vast amount of digital data generated in NICU and PICUs. Nevertheless, the advancement of AI models beyond the developmental and prototyping phase remains exceedingly limited, with only one study describing the clinical evaluation of such a model. Many different aspects required to deploy AI into clinical practice go far beyond the capabilities and responsibilities of most researchers and require great investments in time and resources. To overcome such barriers a structured approach to develop and implement AI models in neonatal and pediatric care could improve this transition. Supplementary Information Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary file1 (DOCX 55 KB) (54KB, docx) Supplementary file2 (XLSX 82 KB) (81.8KB, xlsx) Supplementary file3 (XLSX 56 KB) (56.3KB, xlsx) Acknowledgements The authors wish to thank Maarten Engel from the Erasmus MC Medical Library for developing and updating the search strategy. Author contrib