 service Boston Paris Recife Bogota Liaocheng Chennai a Kumasi Hospitals b 1.47 0.55 1.28 0.67 0.19 1.05 0.44 Hospital beds c (capacity for IV fluids and medications) 241.57 233.81 187.48 128.74 128.97 166.84 72.43 Hospital beds d (with oxygen capacity) 241.57 233.81 187.48 88.56 117.90 153.38 7.27 ICU beds e 18.85 8.38 14.90 11.42 2.80 4.93 0.39 Self-defined ICU beds f 18.85 19.06 14.90 14.59 6.29 12.84 0.69 Ambulances g 27.80 4.20 2.03 3.96 0.77 1.76 0.39 Open in a new tab Includes infrastructure from government and private healthcare facilities with substantial portion of public access a Data represents 73 % hospitals in Chennai. The actual estimates will be higher b Hospitals, with an emergency department, that are part of a system capable of managing acute community acquired conditions, including pediatric hospitals c Number of staffed and available acute care hospital beds with the capacity to deliver IV fluids and medications (for patients admitted from the emergency department) d Number of staffed and available acute care hospital beds with the capacity to deliver oxygen by any delivery system (for patients admitted from the emergency department) e Number of ICU beds with the capacity to deliver oxygen therapy, pulse oximetry, vasopressors, or invasive hemodynamic monitoring (e.g., ability to measure central venous pressure), and invasive mechanical ventilation, and with higher intensity nursing than available on acute care wards f Number of beds classified as ICU beds by the region or the hospital g Vehicles providing services of transporting patients from home to hospital and between hospitals and not used exclusively for transfer between hospitals Local definitions of ICU beds were often more liberal than the standardized definition. According to the standardized definition, ICU beds comprised 3–9 % of all hospital beds in Boston, Bogota, Chennai, Paris, and Recife. ICU bed supply was somewhat lower in Liaocheng, and very low in Kumasi. ICU beds had a more than 45-fold variation, from 0.4 per 100,000 population in Kumasi to 18.8 per 100,000 population in Boston. Boston had a very high supply of ambulances compared to all other cities, while Kumasi had the fewest (>70-fold variation). Data on acute care supply per 100 deaths due to acute illnesses are displayed in Table 4 . Generally, the variation was higher than population-based data. There was a more than 40-fold difference in the availability of hospital beds with capacity for oxygen. The supply of ICU beds varied from less than 1 to 4 per 100 deaths. The supply of ambulances varied from 0.06 to 6.06 per 100 deaths in Kumasi compared to Boston. The supply of hospital beds per 100 deaths due to acute illnesses had a strong positive correlation with national GDP per capita ( r = 0.80, p = 0.03). However, the relationship between ICU bed supply and national GDP per capita was not statistically significant ( r = 0.57, p = 0.18) (Fig. 1 ). We also fitted a non-linear regression line to the non-linear link between hospital beds and per capita GDP that best explained the association between the variables ( R 2 = 0.88, p = 0.01). On the other hand, we did not observe any significant association between ICU beds and GDP by linear ( R 2 = 0.33, p = 0.18) or non-linear analysis ( R 2 = 0.40, p = 0.35). Table 4. Acute care supply per 100 deaths due to acute illnesses Acute care service Boston Paris Recife Bogota Liaocheng Chennai a Kumasi Hospitals b 0.32 0.15 0.32 0.23 0.04 0.20 0.07 Hospital beds c (capacity for IV fluids and medications) 52.66 62.00 46.30 44.70 23.43 27.27 11.67 Hospital beds d (with oxygen capacity) 52.66 62.00 46.30 30.75 23.43 25.07 1.17 ICU beds e 4.11 2.22 3.68 3.96 0.56 0.81 0.06 Self-defined ICU beds f 4.11 5.05 3.68 5.07 1.25 2.10 0.11 Ambulances g 6.06 1.11 0.50 1.38 0.15 0.29 0.06 Open in a new tab Includes infrastructure from government and private healthcare facilities with substantial portion of public access a Data represents 73 % hospitals in Chennai. The actual estimates will be higher b Hospitals, with an emergency department, that are part of a system capable of managing acute community-acquired conditions, including pediatric hospitals c Number of staffed and available acute care hospital beds with the capacity to deliver IV fluids and medications (for patients admitted from the emergency department) d Number of staffed and available acute care hospital beds with the capacity to deliver oxygen by any delivery system (for patients admitted from the emergency department) e Number of ICU beds with the capacity to deliver oxygen therapy, pulse oximetry, vasopressors, or invasive hemodynamic monitoring (e.g., ability to measure central venous pressure), and invasive mechanical ventilation, and with higher intensity nursing than available on acute care wards f Number of beds classified as ICU beds by the region or the hospital g Vehicles providing services of transporting patients from home to hospital and between hospitals and not used exclusively for transfer between hospitals Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Per capita GDP, acute care supply, and population for seven cities. Data points on the bubble chart represent cities’ national GDP per capita in 2011 US$ (based on purchasing power parity) and ratios of acute care supply to 100 deaths due to acute illnesses. The size of the bubble represents city population. Relationship between national GDP per capita and supply of a hospital beds or b ICU beds per 100 deaths due to acute illnesses. Hospital bed supply, as shown by the best-fitting regression curve, was highly associated with per capita GDP ( R 2 = 0.88, p = 0.01), whereas ICU supply was not ( R 2 = 0.33, p = 0.18) Availability and access to data on supply of acute care services Five cities had a centralized data source for at least some data regarding supply of acute care services. For Chennai and Kumasi, all data had to be collected by local investigators directly from acute care institutions (Table 5 ). No city could provide all the information we sought. For example, information regarding ambulances in Paris required contact with each individual fire station. Similarly, we had to contact each hospital in Recife, Bogota, and Liaocheng to determine the number of ICU beds. The richest data for hospital and ICU beds were available for Boston. However, these data were submitted directly by the hospitals to state and federal government with no obvious oversight by city planners. Availability of ambulance data was highly fragmented in most cities. Table 5. Access to information on supply of acute care services Level of data and planning Boston Paris Recife Bogota Liaocheng Chennai Kumasi Centralized data source a Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes No No All data obtained from the centralized source No No No No No No No Identifiable urban authority for acute care services Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes City provided data No b No No No No No No Some or all data collected de novo No Yes Yes Yes No Yes Yes Open in a new tab Data related to, for example, access to information on number of hospitals and number of hospital beds a For example, national or urban healthcare administrative data b Data obtained from Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Service’s Healthcare Cost Report Information System and Massachusetts Office of Emergency Medical Services Discussion We noted three broad findings. First, there was wide variation in the supply of acute care services, such as hospital beds, ICU beds, and ambulances, across the seven cities. That much of the variation correlated with GDP was not surprising. That variation in some aspects of supply, such as ICU care, was not correlated with GDP was surprising, and potentially concerning, especially since the variation was larger when adjusting for burden of disease. Second, not all supply varied widely. For example, several measures of supply were generally similar in both the high- and upper-middle-income cities. Third, much of the data required for the study were not available from urban governments, which was not only challenging for the study, but raises concerns regarding the ability of cities to plan for the future. Variation in the provision of healthcare services is widely reported, both within and across countries. Many countries have large-scale efforts underway to minimize unwanted variation, both to ensure adequate access to vulnerable populations and to avoid excess use of potentially expensive resources. Key to such initiatives is a method to define optimal supply targets. Even within high-income countries, there is no clear agreement on optimal supply of acute care services. For example, we previously reported eightfold variation in ICU beds per population across eight high-income countries, ranging from 3 to 24 beds per 100,000 [ 19 ]. Where ‘optimal’ lies in this range is unclear but it is likely somewhere in the middle: data from the UK, which had three ICU beds per 100,000, suggest ICU supply is too low [ 20 ], whereas the USA, which had a high provision, is struggling to control unsustainably high costs. The relationship between per capita GDP and hospital beds in our sample was not linear, suggesting there may be a point, or asymptote, beyond which greater per capita GDP does not fuel greater supply. Given the small sample, we would urge caution in over-interpreting this relationship until it is validated in a larger study. Whether this relationship suggests that the asymptote represents the optimal supply is also hard to interpret because we do not know whether the relationship is causal. And, of course, as previously noted, there was no relationship between ICU supply and disease burden. Previous ICU-based studies from upper-middle-income countries have often reported higher than expected mortality when compared to cohorts from high-income countries. One possible explanation was that upper-middle-income countries had far fewer ICU beds and therefore restricted ICU admission to much sicker patients. Our data would suggest this explanation seems less likely as the number of ICU beds per capita for upper-middle-income countries fell within the range of high-income countries, and thus raises the importance of determining whether access is equitable or whether the quality of care prior to or during the ICU admission is adequate. Given that ICU facilities are expensive to build and maintain, it would seem imperative to ensure that they are used as wisely as possible. The cities from lower-middle-income countries had a far lower supply of acute care services, and thus these cities should arguably first focus on how and whether to build the volume of these services. As urban populations expand in middle-income countries, access to acute care supply may further deteriorate without prospective planning. Planning, however, will be impossible in the absence of data. Unfortunately, the cities from lower-middle-income countries, which currently have the poorest acute care services supply and are facing the largest population growth projections, were the least likely to provide data. While it is possible that the cities had the data and were unwilling to share, this scenario is unlikely for the following reasons: first, when we could not identify a city source or obtain information from a city source, we performed an extensive online search for urban government or private city websites with information on acute care supply. There was no instance when a city did not provide data, and yet the data were available online. Second, if the city did not have the data, our local investigators collected the data directly. During this process, no local sites told us that they already reported the data to the city. Given that there are no well-recognized metrics for acute care services supply, it is not surprising that cities lack comprehensive data. This lapse is shortsighted given the importance of planning for investment in infrastructure, of which healthcare is an essential pillar. In contrast, data do exist for the supply and demand of other essential urban infrastructures, such as housing, sanitation, and transportation [ 21 – 23 ]. A second threat to effective urban planning for acute care services supply is that healthcare is often not under the direct control of local government, in contrast to other key infrastructure. Thus, cities have to engage in cooperative planning and governance with national or regional governments and with private third parties. Public–private partnerships have been developed to tackle certain specific diseases, such as prevention programs for HIV/AIDS and tuberculosis [ 24 ]. However, whether there could be lessons learned for urban acute care services supply is less clear. Limitations There are several important limitations. First, this study represents an initial attempt to measure urban acute care supply and thus focused on a small convenience sample. The chosen cities may not be representative of their economic regions, and thus we urge caution in extrapolating our findings. Second, there are no standardized approaches for the measurement of acute care services. We therefore chose a set of simple counts of different structural elements (e.g., the number of ambulances or full-service ICU beds). We attempted to standardize their definitions to ensure reliability. However, we cannot comment, for example, on whether the care provided in one ICU is of similar quality or intensity to that provided in another. Also, some services for which we collected data may be able to flux rapidly in response to patients’ needs, such as the number of hospital beds equipped with cylinder-supplied oxygen. Third, even though we standardized definitions of data elements, the method of data collection (e.g., use of existing centralized data vs. manual data collection) varied depending on availability. Although we attempted to verify all data, it is possible that the different methods biased our results. We relied on GBD project data for our measure of disease burden. GBD also suffers from variable data collection methodologies across regions and relies heavily on cause-of-death data, which are prone to error and capture only some aspects of disease burden. Differences in the mortality rate across cities may have led to inaccuracy in projecting the overall burden of a given disease. Furthermore, we selected only those deaths attributed to diseases we considered likely to require acute care. Our selection criteria were based on expert opinion, and are somewhat arbitrary, although we note that others recently proposed a similar approach [ 25 ]. Differences in the age distributions in both of the underlying populations and of the incidence of acute illnesses can have a large effect on estimates of supply. Although we adjusted for age where possible, we note that the age categories available from GBD and census data are broad and finer age categorization may have yielded different estimates. We expressed our counts as overall ratios for the entire urban region. Our findings could be distorted by differences in how urban regions are defined and there could be wide variation in access and supply within any given city. Differences in the ratio of mortality rates between rural and urban areas could also affect our estimates of disease burden. Implications and future research Previous research on supply of acute care services has generally centered on high-income countries and has not focused on the entire spectrum of acute care services supply, from ambulances to ICU beds. Without data, accurate planning for investment in acute care cannot occur. Our results may aid policy makers and researchers regarding decisions on appropriate investments in infrastructure required to meet the acute disease burden in cities, the now dominant location of human habitation. Comparison across cities using standardized data collection tools for acute care supply and estimates of disease burden would help to determine the optimal supply of acute care services. Standardized definitions of the composition, training, and competencies of the workforce providing acute care will further enhance this effort. Conflicts of interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Appendix Footnotes Take-home message: The world is increasingly urbanized. Acute care services are a key part of urban infrastructure, yet there are little data on their distribution. Across 7 cities of diverse economic background, we found up to 100-fold variation in the supply of acute care services, such as hospital beds, ICU beds, and ambulances. Although variation in GDP was correlated with some acute care services, it did not explain the huge variation in ICU supply. Urban governments appeared to have limited knowledge of their acute care services, which raises concern regarding their ability to plan for future population growth. References 1. United Nations Population Fund (2007) Urbanization: a majority in cities. http://www.unfpa.org/pds/urbanization.htm . Accessed 10 July 2012 2. United Nations Population Fund (2008) State of world population 2007: unleashing the potential of urban growth. http://www.unfpa.org/swp/2007/presskit/pdf/sowp2007_eng.pdf . Accessed 10 July 2012 3. Annez PC, Linn JF (2010) Agenda for research on urbanization in developing countries: a summary of findings from a scoping exercise. http://elibrary.worldbank.org/content/workingpaper/10.1596/1813-9450-5476 . Accessed 8 Aug 2012 4. United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs Population Division (2011) Population distribution, urbanization, internal migration, and development: an international perspective. http://www.un.org/esa/population/publications/PopDistribUrbanization/PopulationDistributionUrbanization.pdf . Accessed 8 Aug 2012 5. Firth P, Ttendo S. Intensive care in low-income countries—a critical need. N Engl J Med. 2012;367:1974–1976. doi: 10.1056/NEJMp1204957. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Murthy S, Austin S, Wunsch H, Adhikari NKJ, Karir V, Rowan K, Jacob ST, Salluh J, Bozza F, Du B, An Y, Lee B, Wu F, Oppong C, Venkataraman R, Velayutham V, Angus DC. Variation in acute care burden and supply across diverse urban settings. Crit Care. 2013;17(Suppl 2):P489. [ Google Scholar ] 7. World Health Organization (2008) The global burden of disease: 2004 update. http://www.who.int/healthinfo/global_burden_disease/GBD_report_2004update_full.pdf . Accessed 4 June 2012 8. United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs Population Division (2012) World urbanization prospects: the 2011 revision. http://esa.un.org/unup/Documentation/WUP_glossary.htm . Accessed 12 Nov 2012 9. United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs Population Division (2010) World urbanization prospects: the 2011 revision. http://esa.un.org/unup/unup/index_panel2.html . Accessed 14 Sept 2013 10. United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs Population Division (2011) 2009–2010 UN demographic yearbook. http://unstats.un.org/unsd/demographic/products/dyb/dybsets/2009-2010.pdf . Accessed 14 Sept 2013 11. US Census Bureau (2012) State and county quickfacts. http://quickfacts.census.gov/qfd/states/25000.html . Accessed 14 Sept 2013 12. National Institute of Statistics and Economic Studies (INSEE) (2012) The population census. http://www.insee.fr/ . Accessed 14 Sept 2013 13. City population (2013) Colombia. http://www.citypopulation.de/Colombia.html . Accessed 17 Sept 2013 14. Brazilian Institute of Geography and Statistics (IBGE) (2012) Population estimates. http://www.ibge.gov.br/english/estatistica/populacao/estimativa2012/estimativa_dou.shtm . Accessed 14 Sept 2013 15. Ghana Statistical Services. Social and Demographic Statistics (2010) http://www.statsghana.gov.gh/contact_us.html . Accessed 14 Dec 2011 16. The China Science Center of International Eurasian Academy of Sciences China Association of Mayors UN Habitat (2010) The state of China’s cities 2010/2011. Better city, better life. http://www.unhabitat.org/pmss/listItemDetails.aspx?publicationID=3012 . Accessed 10 July 2013 17. World Health Organization (2013) Global burden of disease (GBD). http://www.who.int/healthinfo/global_burden_disease/en/ . Accessed 10 July 2013 18. The World Bank (2013). GDP per capita, PPP (current international $). http://data.worldbank.org/indicator/NY.GDP.PCAP.PP.CD . Accessed 11 Mar 2013 19. Wunsch H, Angus DC, Harrison DA, Collange O, Fowler R, Hoste EA, de Keizer NF, Kersten A, Linde-Zwirble WT, Sandiumenge A, Rowan KM. Variation in critical care services across North America and Western Europe. Crit Care Med. 2008;36:2787–2793. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0b013e318186aec8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Goldfrad C, Rowan K. Consequences of discharges from intensive care at night. Lancet. 2000;355:1138–1142. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(00)02062-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. The International Bank for Reconstruction and Development/The World Bank (2002) Cities on the move. A World Bank urban transport strategy review. http://siteresources.worldbank.org/INTURBANTRANSPORT/Resources/cities_on_the_move.pdf . Accessed 10 July 2013 22. United Nations Human Settlements Programme (UN-Habitat) (2011) Planning sustainable cities. http://www.unhabitat.org/downloads/docs/GRHS2009/GRHS.2009.pdf . Accessed 10 July 2013 23. World Health Organization and UNICEF (2013) Progress on sanitation and drinking-water—2013 update. http://apps.who.int/iris/bitstream/10665/81245/1/9789241505390_eng.pdf . Accessed 10 July 2013 24. World Health Organization (2009) Investing in our common future. http://www.who.int/pmnch/activities/jointactionplan/100922_2_investing.pdf . Accessed 10 July 2013 25. Hsiao M, Morris SK, Malhotra A, Suraweera W, Jha P. Time-critical mortality conditions in low-income and middle-income countries. Lancet. 2013;381:993–994. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(13)60707-2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',30,'2025-10-15 11:27:19','2025-10-15 11:27:19');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5662,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11042989','The intersection of pediatric anesthesiology and social determinants of health.','Martin SR, Kain ZN.','2024','willer disaggregation decile rented svi svi orellana myourian supracondylar humerus supracondylar humerus takirambudde svi svi mayourian k23hd105042 zeev kain r61mh132249 nichd r01hd091286 niams r01ar073780 r01ca222012 zeev kain mend pacira fortier kain willer mpody nafiu healthypeople tschudy coker aappublications e20153673 gennuso swain catlin jeffries zaslavsky s28 trost ezenwa dankwa yearby reconfiguring kain gahaferi peden diallo heitmiller diallo hasnain wynia ier csdh akbilgic langham mpody willer minneci orellana bpo guidry elfituri zaniletti mayourian javalkar brodeur supracondylar humerus humerus takirambudde ruffolo wakeman pickett nairne nieri luttrell sheyn laverde svi cockrell toolkits nasdoh sokol buckingham acevedo mcardle crisan petterson bazemore 2pt1 hallisey lavery anthropogenic','Abstract Purpose of review: There is increasing evidence of racial and ethnic disparities in pediatric perioperative care, which indicates a need to identify factors driving disparities. Social determinants of health (SDOH) play a fundamental role in pediatric health and are recognized as key underlying mechanisms of health care inequities. This article summarizes recent research exploring the influence of SDOH on pediatric perioperative outcomes. Recent findings: Despite the scarcity of research exploring SDOH and pediatric perioperative outcomes, recent work demonstrates an association between SDOH and multiple outcomes across the perioperative care continuum. Measures of social disadvantage were associated with preoperative symptom severity, longer hospital stays, and higher rates of postoperative complications and mortality. In some studies, these adverse effects of social disadvantage persisted even when controlling for medical comorbidities and clinical severity. Summary: The existing literature offers compelling evidence of the impact of SDOH on perioperative outcomes in children and reveals a critical area in pediatric anesthesia that necessitates further exploration and action. To improve outcomes and address care inequities, future efforts should prioritize the integration of SDOH assessment into pediatric perioperative research and practice. Keywords: anesthesiology, pediatric, perioperative, social determinants of health Introduction There is a growing body of literature documenting pediatric perioperative care disparities and treatment inequities,[ 1 , 2 *] which underscores the need to identify and address factors driving disparities in pediatric perioperative care. To date, evidence of pediatric perioperative disparities has primarily come from research comparing perioperative outcomes across racial and ethnic groups, with data indicating that children of racial and ethnic minoritized groups are more likely to experience inequitable care and poorer outcomes, including more postoperative complications, longer lengths of stay, and inadequate perioperative pain management.[ 1 , 2 *] To identify treatment practices that promote more equitable care and outcomes, it is imperative to appreciate and identify multidimensional, systemic and social mechanisms driving these disparities. Mounting evidence from broader research indicates that social determinants of health (SDOH), or the conditions in which children are born, grow, learn, work, live, and age, and the wider set of social forces shaping daily life [ 3 ], play a fundamental role in pediatric health and health care outcomes.[ 4 – 7 ] SDOH encompass a wide range of social, economic, and environmental factors that can contribute to health benefits or health risks. Secondary to systemic racism and associated inter-generational socioeconomic disadvantage, members of racial and ethnic minoritized groups are more likely to face inequities across SDOH domains.[ 8 , 9 ] Thus, racial health and health care disparities need to be considered in the context of SDoH. In the field of anesthesiology, there have been increasing calls to acknowledge the influence of SDOH on perioperative care and outcomes,[ 10 , 11 ] which is not surprising given the advances in perioperative medicine and the appreciation for a more integrated, patient-centered approach to care across the perioperative continuum. Proponents of incorporating SDOH into research in practice have emphasized the need for a more comprehensive understanding of how social and environmental factors – such as the home environment, access to care and resources, and financial stability – influence preoperative health, perioperative treatment, and postoperative outcomes. While most of the efforts to advance the study of SDOH in perioperative care have stemmed from the adult literature,[ 11 – 13 *] there is a growing body of research in this area. In this article, we will briefly define and frame SDOH in the context of pediatric perioperative care, then review the current literature (i.e., published after June 2022) examining effects of SDOH on pediatric perioperative outcomes, and conclude with a discussion of the implications of recent findings and suggestions for how to further the study of SDOH in pediatric anesthesia to advance equitable care. Of note, this review will present research that goes beyond examining racial disparities (i.e. differences in perioperative outcomes across racial or ethnic groups) and incorporates the measurement of SDOH variables and analyzes the effect of SDOH on perioperative outcomes. For an update on racial disparities in pediatric anesthesia, please see recent reviews by Willer et al.[ 2 *] and Tan et al.[ 1 ] for a comprehensive overview of racial disparities in pediatric anesthesia and perioperative pain management. Social Determinants of Health Social determinants of health (SDOH) are defined as the circumstances in which individuals are born, grow, learn, live, and age, and the wider set of social forces shaping daily life.[ 3 ] SDOH play a significant role in health, development, and health care outcomes [ 4 – 7 ] and are categorized into the following domains: education access and quality, economic stability, social and community context, neighborhood and built environment, and healthcare access and quality (see Figure 1 .[ 14 ] SDOH are particularly relevant for pediatric anesthesia care as social disadvantage has been linked to increase incidence of medical comorbidities associated with perioperative risk (e.g., prematurity, asthma, obesity)[ 15 , 16 ]. Further, other components of SDOH such as financial and housing stability, health literacy, and access to transportation, healthy food, clean air and water, and primary care all likely play a role in a child’s preoperative health and that child and family’s ability to understand, navigate and comply with postoperative instructions and follow-up care. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Social determinants of health domains and examples SDOH across the Perioperative Continuum As illustrated in the sections below, there is limited research examining the impact of SDOH in pediatric perioperative outcomes; however, the existing literature provides strong initial evidence of the influence of SDOH on pediatric perioperative outcome disparities. The review of current literature is organized by perioperative period, ranging from studies assessing preoperative outcomes to postoperative outcomes. Although SDOH assessment methodology varied across studies, most studies utilized a neighborhood-level mapping measure that derives a composite score of social disadvantage from neighborhood-level, census track indicators (e.g., employment, poverty, education, and housing quality). Given the variability in measures used to assess social disadvantage, measure descriptions are presented in Table 1 and not in the text below. Table 1. Area-Level Indices of Social Disadvantage Index Level of Disaggregation Components Measured Index Score(s) Area Deprivation Index (ADI)[ 29 ] Census block group 17 measures across five domains: income, education, housing, employment, and household characteristics State Decile and National Percentile (1 to 10 [state] or 100 [national]); higher scores = higher disadvantage Child Opportunity Index (COI)[ 30 ] Census tract 29 measures across 3 domains: education, health and environment, and social and economic Overall COI and domain scores (1–100) and national quintiles; lower scores = higher disadvantage Social Deprivation Index (SDI)[ 31 ] Census tract, Zip Code Tabulation Area 7 measures related to poverty, unemployment, education, overcrowding, access to a vehicle, single-parent households, and rented housing Overall SDI score (0–100); higher scores = higher disadvantage Social Vulnerability Index (SVI) [ 32 ] Census tract 15 measures across four domains: socioeconomic, household composition and disability, minority status and language, and housing type and transportation Overall SVI and four domain percentile ranks (0–1.00); higher percentiles = higher social vulnerability Open in a new tab SDOH and the Preoperative Period Three recent studies have explored the effect of SDOH on preoperative outcomes. Two of which included pediatric patients undergoing scoliosis surgery and used neighborhood-level measures of social disadvantage. Orellana et al.[ 17 **] found that higher social disadvantage, as measured by the Child Opportunity Index (COI), was associated with more severe scoliosis at pre-surgical evaluation. In another study, social disadvantage was associated with preoperative appointment attendance such that patients living in more disadvantaged neighborhoods were more likely to miss preoperative appointments. Results of another study that assessed the influence of socioeconomic factors (i.e., income and insurance) on pre-surgical symptom severity in children undergoing surgery for Chiari malformation type I showed an association between lower household income and higher levels of pre-surgical pain and more severe Chiari-related symptoms.[ 18 **] SDOH and Operative and In-Hospital Period The role of SDOH on surgical and in-hospital outcomes has been explored in samples of children undergoing surgery for congenital heart disease, orthopedic surgery, or appendectomy. In children undergoing congenital heart disease surgery, higher social disadvantage, as measured by the COI, was shown to be associated with longer length of stay and likelihood of in-hospital mortality.[ 19 **, 20 **] Myourian et al. conducted additional analyses to identify neighborhood and patient-level variables that accounted for the association between social disadvantage and length of stay. Results indicated that neighborhood level indices of food and housing insecurity, lack of health insurance, lower socioeconomic status, and lower parental literacy and education were associated with longer hospital length of stay. These associations persisted even when controlling for medical complexity and mortality risk and neighborhood level factors were also associated with poorer postoperative outcomes, which will be discussed in the following section. A connection between SDOH and surgical treatment and in-hospital outcomes has also been reported in pediatric patients undergoing surgery for supracondylar humerus fractures, appendectomy, and tonsil and adenoidectomy. Modest et al.[ 21 *] assessed whether social disadvantage, as measured by the Social Deprivation Index (SDI), was associated with likelihood of outpatient vs. inpatient surgical management of pediatric supracondylar humerus fractures and higher social deprivation was associated with increased odds of requiring inpatient surgery. However, fracture type nor severity was accounted for in study analyses, which may have influenced the surgical admission decision. Takirambudde et al.[ 22 **] created a composite score based on the Area Deprivation Index (ADI) and SDI and conducted factor analyses to identify latent neighborhood-level factors associated with length of stay and post-surgical procedures in children with complicated appendicitis undergoing appendectomy. In this study, the neighborhood-level factor that captured percent of Hispanic and foreign-born residents was found to correlate with length of stay and increased instance of percutaneous drainage and post-surgical imaging. In children undergoing tonsil and adenoidectomy, those with the greatest social disadvantage (i.e., highest Social Vulnerability Index (SVI) quartile) were more likely to require prolonged oxygen and overnight monitoring, controlling for asthma and preoperative oxygen level.[ 23 *] SDOH and the Postoperative Period The majority or research reviewed on SDOH in pediatric perioperative outcomes focused on the role of SDOH in postoperative outcomes and more specifically post-discharge outcomes. Across studies, outcomes associated with SDOH in both relatively healthy samples and those with chronic illness included post-discharge complications, readmissions, and mortality. In healthy children and those undergoing elective tonsil and adenoidectomy surgery, higher indices of social disadvantage, as measured by SVI, were associated with postoperative complications (e.g., bleeding, respiratory distress, death) and re-admission within 30 days.[ 24 **, 25 **] In the tonsil and adenoidectomy, social disadvantage remained a significant correlate of postoperative complications and readmission even when controlling for medical comorbidities (e.g., obesity, asthma, obstructive sleep apnea). Further analyses of social disadvantage themes identified high socioeconomic vulnerability and high house composition vulnerability as unique predictors of postoperative complications and high housing and transportation vulnerability as unique predictors of readmission.[ 24 **] In another large retrospective analysis of patients 21 years old and younger undergoing a variety of surgical procedures, ADI-measured social disadvantage was associated with increased incidence of postoperative mortality, serious adverse events, and readmission even when controlling for patient demographic (e.g., age, race, ethnicity) and clinical variables (e.g., ASA classification and surgery duration).[ 26 **] Previously introduced studies assessing SDOH in pediatric patients undergoing congenital heart disease surgery[ 19 **, 20 **] also examined effects of social disadvantage on post-surgical mortality and survival rates. Results of this work revealed a positive association between COI-measured social disadvantage and increased risk for mortality and poorer survival rates. Results from Duong et al.[ 19 **] suggest that those in the lowest COI quintile (i.e., highest level of social disadvantage) may be at particularly high risk of poorer outcomes. Further, results from neighborhood and patient-level analyses in the Mayourian et al.[ 20 **] study revealed that neighborhood-level indices associated with longer length of stay (i.e., food and housing insecurity, lack of health insurance, lower socioeconomic status, and lower parental literacy and education) were also associated with increased risk for mortality. At the patient-level, children with public insurance and those with Spanish-speaking caregivers had higher mortality rates.[ 20 **] Conclusion The recent literature, although limited, provides evidence of the influence of SDOH on perioperative outcomes in children, revealing a critical area in pediatric anesthesia that necessitates further exploration and action. Moreover, results suggest that the effects of SDOH span across the entire perioperative continuum of care. From the preoperative period, where factors like social disadvantage can impact preoperative appointment attendance and symptom severity, to the operative and in-hospital period, to the operative and in-hospital period, where social disadvantage can influence the length of stay and likelihood of in-hospital mortality. The postoperative period is also significantly affected, with a variety of indicators of social disadvantage increasing likelihood of postoperative complications, readmissions, and mortality. In the majority of studies reviewed, these deleterious effects of social disadvantage persisted even after controlling for medical comorbidities and anesthesia risk factors (e.g., obesity, asthma). Thus, underscoring the need for pediatric anesthesiologists to be aware of and responsive to nonmedical, social factors that may affect children’s perioperative health and recovery. Given the potential impact of SDOH on pediatric perioperative outcomes, assessment and screening may represent an important first to step to identifying and mitigating risk. Although there is no consensus on a standard tool to assess SDOH, most of the studies cited above used a neighborhood mapping measure that derived a social disadvantage score from the patient’s street level address. These tools are widely accessible and could be integrated into the preoperative evaluation using data from the electronic medical record. Results also indicated that certain SDOH factors, such as food and housing insecurity, access to health care, and language fluency, may place children at a higher risk for poorer outcomes. Implementation of a SDOH screening tool that identifies unmet social needs may inform more personalized preoperative care plans and referrals to appropriate resources. Indeed, there are increasing calls to require SDOH screening in pediatrics and a variety of standardized SDOH screening tools have been developed for pediatric clinical care settings.[ 27 , 28 ] The limitations of current research may also inform future directions in the study of SDOH and pediatric anesthesia. Specifically, most studies included retrospective cohort samples and few included patient-reported outcomes. Prospective, longitudinal studies incorporating patient or family-reported outcomes are needed to better identify patient-centered intervention targets and appropriate points of intervention. In addition, incorporating the assessment of both risk and protective factors may facilitate the development of more equitable interventions that incorporate patient’s strengths and resources. Further, intervention studies and research employing randomized-controlled trials are needed to inform the development and implementation of effective interventions. The current body of research, while still growing, offers compelling evidence of the impact of SDOH on perioperative outcomes in children. Collectively, findings suggest that SDOH influence outcomes across the perioperative care continuum and these effects persist even when controlling for medical comorbidities. The emerging data underscore the need to integrate SDOH into pediatric perioperative research and practice as this integration presents an opportunity to improve health outcomes and address existing inequities. Key points: The prevalence of racial and ethnic disparities in pediatric perioperative care underscores a need to move beyond documenting racial differences and identify and address systemic and social mechanisms likely driving disparities. Social determinants of health (SDOH) is an understudied topic in pediatric anesthesia, but a growing body of research suggests that SDOH influence outcomes across the perioperative continuum. Collectively, results indicate that social disadvantage is associated with poorer perioperative outcomes even when accounting for medical comorbidities and clinical severity, emphasizing the need to consider the impact of SDOH in pediatric perioperative care. Incorporating SDOH assessment into preoperative evaluations may help identify risks and inform more personalized and equitable perioperative care plans. The current research, predominantly retrospective, points to the need for more longitudinal studies incorporating patient-reported data to identify intervention targets and inform the development of more effective and equitable care practices. 1. Acknowledgments: 2. Financial support: Sarah Martin is supported by the National Institutes of Health National Institute for Child Health and Human Development (K23HD105042, PI: Martin). Zeev Kain is supported by the National Institutes of Health (NIMH R61MH132249, PI; NICHD R01HD091286 PI; NIAMS R01AR073780, Site PI; NCI R01CA222012, Co-I). Footnotes 3. Conflicts of Interest: Zeev Kain is the president of the American College of Perioperative Medicine and currently serves as a consultant for Mend and Pacira. The other authors have no other conflicts of interest to disclose. References 1. Tan H, Mendoza BA, Fortier MA, Kain Z. Perioperative pain disparity in children: A call for action. Pediatric Anesthesia. 2022;32(12):1365–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Willer BL, Mpody C, Nafiu OO. Racial Inequity in Pediatric Anesthesia. Curr Anesthesiol Rep. 2023. May 1;13(2):108–16. * This review of recent research on racial disparities in pediatric perioperative care summarizes the prevalence of racial disparities across surgical specialties and highlights the need to examine underlying mechanisms of disparities such as SDOH. 3. Healthy People 2030. Social Determinants of Health - Healthy People 2030 | health.gov [Internet]. U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of Disease Prevention and Health Promotion; [cited 2023 Jun 3]. Available from: https://health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health [ Google Scholar ] 4. Beck AF, Tschudy MM, Coker TR, et al. Determinants of Health and Pediatric Primary Care Practices. Pediatrics [Internet]. 2016. Mar 1 [cited 2020 Oct 15];137(3). Available from: https://pediatrics.aappublications.org/content/137/3/e20153673 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 5. Hood CM, Gennuso KP, Swain GR, Catlin BB. County health rankings: relationships between determinant factors and health outcomes. American Journal of Preventive Medicine. 2016;50(2):129–35. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Jeffries N, Zaslavsky AM, Diez Roux AV, et al. Methodological approaches to understanding causes of health disparities. American journal of public health. 2019;109(S1):S28–33. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Mathur VA, Trost Z, Ezenwa MO, et al. Mechanisms of injustice: what we (do not) know about racialized disparities in pain. Pain. 2022;163(6):999. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Dankwa-Mullan I, Rhee KB, Williams K, et al. The Science of Eliminating Health Disparities: Summary and Analysis of the NIH Summit Recommendations. Am J Public Health. 2010. Apr;100(S1):S12–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Yearby R. Structural Racism and Health Disparities: Reconfiguring the Social Determinants of Health Framework to Include the Root Cause. Journal of Law, Medicine & Ethics. 2020. Oct;48(3):518–26. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Kain ZN, Gahaferi AA, Peden CJ. Closing the Knowledge Translation Gap: Health Services Research and Perioperative Medicine—New Horizons for Anesthesiologists. Anesthesia & Analgesia. 2022. Mar;134(3):441. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Tan JM. Uncovering a Critical Link: Social and Environmental Determinants of Health in Patient Safety. ASA Monitor. 2023. Nov 1;87(11):37. [ Google Scholar ] 12. Diallo MS, Tan JM, Heitmiller ES, Vetter TR. Achieving Greater Health Equity: An Opportunity for Anesthesiology. Anesthesia & Analgesia. 2022. Jun;134(6):1175. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Diallo MS, Hasnain-Wynia R, Vetter TR. Social Determinants of Health and Preoperative Care. Anesthesiology Clinics. 2023. Aug 25;0(0). * Although focused more on the adult population, this paper outlines the role of SDOH in preoperative health, proposing approaches that could be applicable to pediatric perioperative care. 14. Closing the gap in a generation: health equity through action on the social determinants of health - Final report of the commission on social determinants of health [Internet]. [cited 2024 Jan 10]. Available from: https://www.who.int/publications-detail-redirect/WHO-IER-CSDH-08.1 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 15. Akbilgic O, Langham MR, Davis RL. Race, preoperative risk factors, and death after surgery. Pediatrics. 2018;141(2). [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Mpody C, Willer BL, Minneci PC, et al. Moderating effects of race and preoperative comorbidity on surgical mortality in infants. Journal of Surgical Research. 2021;264:435–43. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Orellana KJ, Lee J, Yang D, et al. Impact of Social Determinants of Health on Adolescent Idiopathic Scoliosis Curve Severity. Journal of Pediatric Orthopaedics. 2023; 10.1097/BPO.0000000000002529. ** This retrospective study describes the association between SDOH and adolescent idiopathic scoliosis severity at pre-surgical evaluation. These results provide evidence for the impact of SDOH on preoperative severity in this patient population and may inform preventive treatment. 18. Kelly KA, Guidry BS, Wong GW, et al. The role of traditional social determinants of health in referral patterns, timing of surgery, and Chiari Health Index for Pediatrics–determined quality of life in children with symptomatic Chiari type I malformation. Journal of Neurosurgery: Pediatrics. 2023. Sep 15;32(6):686–91. ** This retrospective study found that lower family income, but not insurance type, was associated with more severe pre-surgical pain and symptoms. These findings may have implications for post-surgical pain and recovery in patients with Chiari type I malformation and results, including non-significant findings may help inform future research in this area. 19. Duong SQ, Elfituri MO, Zaniletti I, et al. Neighborhood Childhood Opportunity, Race/Ethnicity, and Surgical Outcomes in Children With Congenital Heart Disease. Journal of the American College of Cardiology. 2023. Aug 29;82(9):801–13. ** In this study, children undergoing heart surgery who had the highest social disadvantage had increased incidence of in-hospital mortality. The use of mediation analyses in this study allowed for exploring mechanisms underlying disparities and indicated that social disadvantage may partially mediate racial disparities in mortality rates, demonstrating. 20. Mayourian J, Brown E, Javalkar K, et al. Insight into the Role of the Child Opportunity Index on Surgical Outcomes in Congenital Heart Disease. The Journal of Pediatrics. 2023. Aug 1;259:113464. ** Study results demonstrate the effect of higher social disadvantage on poorer in-hospital and postoperative outcomes following heart surgery, accounting for medical comorbidities. This study adds important evidence of SDOH affecting in-hospital and postoperative outcomes, and the examination of neighborhood- and patient-level correlates helps identify intervention targets. 21. Modest JM, Brodeur PG, Kim KW, et al. Social Disparities in Outpatient and Inpatient Management of Pediatric Supracondylar Humerus Fractures. Journal of Clinical Medicine. 2022. Jan;11(15):4573. ** This study demonstrates that SDOH may play a role in the surgical management of humerus fractures (outpatient vs. inpatient) in children and adds to the limited research on SDOH in the pediatric orthopedic care literature. 22. Takirambudde S, Ruffolo LI, Wakeman DS, et al. Social Determinants of Health are Associated With Postoperative Outcomes in Children With Complicated Appendicitis. Journal of Surgical Research. 2022. Nov 1;279:692–701. ** Through the use of a novel SDOH composite score and subsequent factor analyses, this study identified neighborhood-level factors (prevalence of Hispanic ethnicity and foreign-born individuals) as unique contributors to length of stay and post-operative outcomes in children with complicated appendicitis. The study results and methodology could inform more nuanced assessment and analyses of the effects of SDOH. 23. Goff SH, Pickett‐Nairne K, Nguyen T, et al. The Effect of Social Vulnerability on Perioperative Tonsillectomy Outcomes in Children. The Laryngoscope. 2023. Nov 16;lary.31189. * The results of this study, which showed that children with the greatest social disadvantage were more likely to require prolonged oxygen monitoring, demonstrate the utility of SDOH screening and may help inform improvements in perioperative treatment planning for T&A. 24. Nieri CA, Davies C, Luttrell JB, Sheyn A. Associations Between Social Vulnerability Indicators and Pediatric Tonsillectomy Outcomes. Laryngoscope. 2023; ** Results from this study demonstrate that the negative effects of social disadvantage on post-operative outcomes are present in healthy children. Analyses of social disadvantage themes revealed unique predictors of postoperative complications and readmission, identifying targets for future research. 25. Yap A, Laverde R, Thompson A, et al. Social vulnerability index (SVI) and poor postoperative outcomes in children undergoing surgery in California. Am J Surg. 2023. Jan;225(1):122–8. ** This study provides further evidence of the impact of SDOH on postoperative outcomes in a large sample of healthy children undergoing various surgical procedures, demonstrating that higher social disadvantage was associated with serious postoperative complications. 26. Cockrell H, Barry D, Dick A, Greenberg S. Geographic access to care and pediatric surgical outcomes. The American Journal of Surgery. 2023;225(5):903–8. ** This retrospective study sampled pediatric patients with a range of clinical severity undergoing a variety of surgical procedures. Controlling for patient demographics, clinical, and treatment variables, social disadvantage was associated with poorer postoperative outcomes, demonstrating the impact of SDOH above and beyond clinical and treatment severity. 27. Screening Tools and Toolkits [Internet]. National Alliance to Impact the Social Determinants of Health. [cited 2023 Oct 11]. Available from: https://nasdoh.org/screening-tools-and-tool-kits/ 28. Sokol R, Austin A, Chandler C, et al. Screening Children for Social Determinants of Health: A Systematic Review. Pediatrics. 2019;144(4). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Kind AJ, Buckingham WR. Making neighborhood-disadvantage metrics accessible—the neighborhood atlas. N Engl J Med. 2018;378(26):2456–2458. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Acevedo-Garcia D, McArdle N, Hardy EF, Crisan UI, Romano B, Norris D, et al. The Child Opportunity Index: Improving Collaboration Between Community Development And Public Health. Health Aff (Millwood). 2014. Nov;33(11):1948–57. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Butler DC, Petterson S, Phillips RL, Bazemore AW. Measures of Social Deprivation That Predict Health Care Access and Need within a Rational Area of Primary Care Service Delivery. Health Serv Res. 2013. Apr;48(2pt1):539–59. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. Flanagan BE, Hallisey EJ, Adams E, Lavery A. Measuring community vulnerability to natural and anthropogenic hazards: the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention’s Social Vulnerability Index. J Environ Health. 2018;80(10):34–36. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',4,'2025-10-15 11:27:30','2025-10-15 11:27:30');
