INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5675,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11449137','Grey matter ageing-related tau astrogliopathy: associations with brain pathologies and cognitive decline.','Agrawal S, Yu L, Leurgans SE, Kapasi A, Barnes LL, Bennett DA, Boyle PA, Schneider JA.','2024','ramified tsas gfa enrols priests nuns enrols bisected interhemispheric bisecting slabs photographed slabs tsas gfas thermoscientific tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas severities tsas tsas gfas gfas bielschowsky covance 10d5 elan millimetre stereological tar5p 1d3 haematoxylin zymed wako tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas tsas gfas polychoric polychoric polychotomous tsas gfas pleiotropy harbours furthers aquaporin aquaporin glymphatic aquaporin awae250_supplementary_data awae250_supplementary_data srabani mondal poirier colvin tracey nowakowski shayla calloway sonal alifiya r01ag067482 r01ag017917 p30ag010161 r01ag022018 r01ag015819 r01ag064233 r01ag034374 r01ag033678 ghebremedhin resende epf e1353 e1364 s161 s189 segawa sytsma conscientiousness drachman katzman stadlan nincds adrda unravels resende latimer stair hincks bigenic dmm049323 montalbano mcallen cascio kril bachstetter bieniek nibib mlmd maphis kokiko yoshiyama merlini ulmann nitsch arcaβ bellucci westwood casamenti spillantini iliff plog glymphatic yousef kaindl aquaporin arighi arcaro fumagalli aquaporin glymphatic aquaporin glymphatic naus connexin43 pannexin1 awae250_supplementary_data awae250_supplementary_data','Abstract Grey matter ageing-related tau astrogliopathy (ARTAG) pathology is common in aged brains and detected in multiple brain regions. However, the associations of grey matter ARTAG with Alzheimer''s disease and other common age-related proteinopathies, in addition to clinical phenotypes, including Alzheimer''s dementia and cognitive decline, remain unclear. We examined 442 decedents (mean age at death = 90 years, males = 32%) from three longitudinal community-based clinical–pathological studies. Using AT8 immunohistochemistry, grey matter ARTAG pathology was counted in the superior frontal region, anterior temporal tip and amygdala and summarized as a severity score ranging from zero (none) to six (severe). Alzheimer''s disease and other common age-related neuropathologies were also evaluated. The diagnosis of Alzheimer''s dementia was based on clinical evaluations; annual tests of cognitive performance were summarized as global cognition and five cognitive domains. Multivariable logistic regression tested the associations of grey matter ARTAG pathology with an array of age-related neuropathologies. To evaluate associations of grey matter ARTAG pathology with Alzheimer''s dementia and cognitive decline, we used logistic regression and linear mixed-effect models. Grey matter ARTAG pathology was seen in 324 (73%) participants, of which 303 (68%) participants had ARTAG in the amygdala, 246 (56%) in the anterior temporal tip and 137 (31%) in the superior frontal region. Grey matter ARTAG pathology from each of the three regions was associated with a pathological diagnosis of Alzheimer''s disease and limbic-predominant age-related TAR DNA-binding protein 43 encephalopathy–neuropathological change but not with vascular pathology. In fully adjusted models that controlled for demographics, Alzheimer''s disease and common age-related pathologies, an increase in severity of grey matter ARTAG pathology in the superior frontal cortex, but not in the amygdala or the anterior temporal tip, was associated with higher odds of Alzheimer''s dementia and faster decline in global cognition, episodic memory and semantic memory. These results provide compelling evidence that grey matter ARTAG, specifically in the superior frontal cortex, contributes to Alzheimer''s dementia and cognitive decline in old age. Keywords: ageing, ageing-related tau astrogliopathy, Alzheimer’s disease pathology, cognition, dementia, TAR DNA-binding protein 43 Ageing-related tau astrogliopathy (ARTAG) is a 4R tau astrocytic pathology. Agrawal et al. study grey matter ARTAG pathology in community-dwelling older adults and conclude that ARTAG in superior frontal cortex increases the risk of Alzheimer''s dementia and is associated with faster cognitive decline. Introduction Ageing-related tau astrogliopathy (ARTAG) is a 4R tau astrocytic pathology, distinct from other astroglial tau lesions including astrocytic plaques, tufted astrocytes, ramified astrocytes or globular astroglial inclusions. 1 It was first described by Ikeda et al . 2 as tau-positive thorn-shaped astrocytes. In 2016, an international group of neuropathologists published a strategy to harmonize the evaluation and nomenclature of ARTAG 1 and classified ARTAG into two discrete morphologies, i.e. thorn-shaped astrocytes (TSAs) and granular/fuzzy astrocytes (GFA), which can accumulate in multiple locations of the brain, including the white matter, grey matter, subependymal, subpial and perivascular area. 1 , 3 Many studies have shown that ARTAG is a common pathology in the brains of older persons, >60 years of age 4 , 5 ; however, the independent association of ARTAG with late-life cognitive outcomes is unclear. Available data are limited but might suggest that ARTAG increases the odds of Alzheimer''s dementia and cognitive impairment, 6 , 7 although little is known about the associations of ARTAG with cognitive decline above and beyond other brain pathologies. Moreover, limited findings are reported on the association of ARTAG with Alzheimer’s disease (AD) and other brain pathologies including limbic-predominant age-related TAR DNA-binding protein 43 (TDP-43) encephalopathy–neuropathological change (LATE-NC) and vascular pathologies. 6 , 8 , 9 In this study, we examined ARTAG pathology from the grey matter location of the amygdala and two neocortical regions (anterior temporal tip and superior frontal cortex). Decedents came from one of three longitudinal community-based cohort studies of ageing who completed annual cognitive testing for an average of 10.6 years and who died and came to autopsy. The neuropathological examination included measures of grey matter ARTAG, AD and other neurodegenerative and vascular pathologies from multiple brain regions. Initially, we studied whether grey matter ARTAG is associated with AD and other brain pathologies, then we examined the associations of grey matter ARTAG pathology with Alzheimer''s dementia and cognitive decline. Materials and methods Participants Participants came from one of three ongoing cohort studies of ageing: the Religious Orders Study (ROS), the Rush Memory and Aging Project (MAP) and the Minority Aging Research Study (MARS). ROS began in 1994 and enrols older Catholic priests, nuns and brothers throughout the USA. MAP started in 1997 and recruits older persons from the greater Chicago metropolitan area, and MARS started in 2004 and enrols older participants who self-identify as African American from the greater Chicago metropolitan area. 10 , 11 All participants were recruited without known dementia and agreed to annual clinical evaluation and cognitive testing. All three studies were approved by the Institutional Review Board of Rush University Medical Center. All participants in ROS and MAP and a subset of participants of MARS provided informed consent and agreed to brain donation and signed the Anatomical Gift Act. At the time of these analyses, 4576 participants had consented, of whom 4363 completed the baseline evaluation. Of those, 4015 had longitudinal cognitive data, 2329 participants had died, 1877 underwent brain autopsies, and 1786 had a neuropathological evaluation approved by neuropathologists with expertise in ageing and dementia. ARTAG data collection was started in 2020 from a consecutive subset of deceased and autopsied participants. There were 442 decedents with valid ARTAG pathology data available at the time of analyses from 1786 participants who were included ( Supplementary Fig. 1 ). The subset of 442 were similar to decedents who did not have ARTAG data ( n = 1344) in terms of demographics, Alzheimer''s dementia and AD pathology status ( Supplementary Table 1 ). Clinical evaluation and cognitive testing Participants from all three studies underwent a uniform structured evaluation including neuropsychological testing and neurological examination at baseline and annual follow-ups. 12 They undertook a uniform battery of 21 tests, 19 of which were used to assess global cognitive function and five specific cognitive domains including episodic, semantic and working memory, perceptual speed and visuospatial ability. Seven of the 19 neuropsychological tests were used to measure episodic memory, three tests for semantic memory, three tests for working memory, four tests for perceptual speed and two tests for visuospatial ability. The summary scores for each cognitive domain were computed by standardizing raw scores of individual cognitive tests using the baseline mean and standard deviation (SD) of all participants, then averaging across the tests specific to each domain. Finally, z -scores of all 19 tests were averaged to construct an overall score of global cognition, with lower scores indicating poorer cognitive performance. 13-15 To determine Alzheimer''s dementia at the time of a participant''s death, all available clinical records were reviewed by an experienced clinician who classified the dementia status of participants using recommendations of the joint working group of the National Institute of Neurologic and Communicative Disorders and Stroke and the Alzheimer''s Disease and Related Disorders Association. 16 Briefly, a diagnosis of Alzheimer''s dementia was rendered if a participant had a history of cognitive decline and impairment in memory and at least one other cognitive domain. Biomarkers were not considered when determining the diagnosis of Alzheimer''s dementia in this study. Neuropathological evaluation The median post-mortem interval was 7.3 (interquartile range = 5.4–12.5) h. In brief, the brain was bisected into hemispheres through the interhemispheric cerebral fissure, bisecting the corpus callosum. The hemisphere exhibiting significant gross pathology with minimal mechanical damage was chosen for fixation. If pathology was present in both hemispheres, the one with the most widespread pathology was selected for fixation, with representative pathology tissue from the other hemisphere also fixed and the remaining tissue from other side frozen. In cases where no gross pathology was observed in either hemisphere, one hemisphere was chosen arbitrarily for fixation. The selected hemisphere underwent fixation in 4% paraformaldehyde for ≥1 month, while the other hemisphere was immediately sectioned into coronal 1-cm slabs, photographed, and frozen for molecular analysis and tissue distribution purposes. After 1 month of fixation, the fixed hemisphere was sectioned into coronal 1-cm slabs, and at least nine regions (including middle temporal, mid frontal, inferior parietal, entorhinal and anterior cingulate cortices, hippocampus, basal ganglia, thalamus and midbrain) were dissected for further analysis. Additional tissue blocks were taken for any suspected macroscopic infarcts observed during gross examination. All the standard and suspected blocks were embedded in paraffin, cut into 6-µm-thick sections and stained with appropriate stains or antibodies to identify age-related pathologies. All the neuropathological measures were reviewed by neuropathologists with expertise in ageing and dementia, blinded to clinical information. Grey matter ARTAG Based on prior research on ARTAG, 1 , 6 , 17 we examined ARTAG, including TSAs and GFAs, from the grey matter location of three brain regions: the amygdala, anterior temporal tip and superior frontal cortex. Immunostaining was performed on 6-μm-thick sections using a monoclonal antibody specific to phosphorylated-tau AT8 (dilution: 1:3000, ThermoScientific). The numbers of TSAs and, separately, GFAs were counted in a 0.25 mm 2 area of greatest density in each grey matter area and subsequently converted to a six-point scale [none (0), sparse (1–2 TSAs/GFAs), sparse to moderate (3–5 TSAs/GFAs), moderate (6–12 TSAs/GFAs), moderate to severe (13–19 TSAs/GFAs) and severe (≥20 TSAs/GFAs)]. For statistical analyses, a semi-quantitative three-level rating system (0 = none, 1 = mild and 2 = moderate–severe) was created to define grey matter ARTAG in the amygdala and two neocortical regions [0 (none), no TSAs and GFAs in grey matter were observed; 1 (mild), a sparse number of TSAs or GFAs (1–5); and 2 (moderate–severe), a moderate-to-severe number of TSAs or GFAs (≥6)]. Note that ARTAG present in the subpial location was not included in the measure of grey matter ARTAG. Given the potential challenges associated with counting ARTAG measures from AT8-stained tissue, particularly owing to the presence of concomitant neuronal tau tangles and other astrocytic tau pathology, we assessed the inter-rater reliability for TSAs and GFAs in both the amygdala and superior frontal cortex. This evaluation was carried out for 38 cases selected to represent all severities of grey matter ARTAG. The weighted Kappa statistics were 0.89 for the severity of superior frontal cortex TSAs, 0.78 for amygdala TSAs, 0.94 for superior frontal cortex GFAs and 0.73 for amygdala GFAs, indicating moderate to excellent agreement. Alzheimer''s disease pathology AD pathology, including neuritic and diffuse plaques and neurofibrillary tangles, was identified from 6-µm-thick modified Bielschowsky silver-stained sections of midfrontal, middle temporal, entorhinal and inferior parietal cortices and the CA1 sector of the hippocampus. Each pathological feature (neuritic plaques, diffuse plaques and tangles) was counted manually in a 1 mm 2 area of greatest density from five brain regions, resulting in 15 separate pathological measures for each participant. As previously described, we first scaled the raw counts of each by dividing the raw count for each individual by the SD for that particular count. Next, we averaged the scaled scores for each pathological index from the five cortical regions to form summary measures of neuritic plaques, diffuse plaques and neurofibrillary tangles. A global summary measure of AD pathology was then computed by averaging the summary measures of diffuse plaques, neuritic plaques and neurofibrillary tangles. 18 This global AD pathology summary measure, indicating the overall burden of AD pathology, has been widely used in earlier clinical–pathological studies 19-21 and shown to be a reliable method for summarizing the overall AD pathology burden by comparing it with other conventional AD methods, including the Consortium to Establish a Registry for Alzheimer''s Disease (CERAD) system, Braak staging and National Institute on Aging–Reagan criteria. 22 The range of the global AD pathology summary measure among the 442 participants was 0–3.06 units. 18 The National Institute on Aging–Alzheimer''s Association (NIA-AA) criteria based on Braak, CERAD and Thal stages were used to determine the pathological diagnosis of AD, where intermediate or high Alzheimer''s disease neuropathological change (ADNC) corresponds to a pathological diagnosis of AD and no or low ADNC indicates no pathological AD. 23 Additional evaluations were performed to quantify the region-specific amyloid-β and tau tangle burden. For the assessment of amyloid-β load, sections of the superior frontal cortex and amygdala were immunohistochemically stained with antibodies against Aβ, 4G8 (1:9000; Covance Labs), 6F/3D (1:50; Dako) and 10D5 (1:600; Elan Pharmaceuticals). An experienced rater delineated the cortex, applied a custom positive-pixel algorithm created by ImageJ software, and the value of region-specific amyloid-β was assessed as the percentage area of the cortex occupied by amyloid-β deposits, as previously described. 24 For assessment of tau-tangle burden, sections of the superior frontal cortex and amygdala were stained with the AT8 antibody (1:2000; Thermo Scientific), and the regional density of tau tangles per square millimetre was quantified using stereological mapping software by a proficient and experienced rater. 24 Other neurodegenerative and vascular pathologies A range of non-AD neurodegenerative [LATE-NC and Lewy bodies (LBs)] and vascular (arteriolosclerosis, atherosclerosis, cerebral amyloid angiopathy and cerebral infarcts) pathologies were assessed systematically. In brief, LATE-NC pathology (TDP-43 cytoplasmic inclusions) was assessed from eight brain regions using phosphorylated monoclonal TAR5P-1D3 anti-TDP-43 antibody and rated into four stages based on the presence of TDP-43 cytoplasmic inclusions in the brain. LATE-NC stage 0 denotes an absence of TDP-43 pathology. Stage 1 encompasses cases where TDP-43 is present solely in the amygdala. Stage 2 involves cases with TDP-43 pathology extending into the entorhinal cortex or hippocampus. Lastly, stage 3 identifies cases with TDP-43 pathology extending into any neocortical regions, including the midfrontal, middle temporal, anterior temporal and inferior orbital frontal cortices. A dichotomous measure (stage 0 or 1 versus stage 2 or 3) was used for analysis. 25 , 26 Hippocampal sclerosis (HS) was identified on the 6-μm-thick haematoxylin- and eosin-stained sections from the mid-hippocampus based on the presence of severe neuronal loss and gliosis in CA1 and/or the subiculum. 25 The presence of LBs was examined from neocortical (midfrontal, inferior parietal and middle temporal), limbic (cingulate and entorhinal cortices and amygdala) and substantia nigra regions with monoclonal antibodies specific to phosphorylated α-synuclein (clone LB 509; 1:50; Zymed Labs, Invitrogen Corp. or clone pSyn#64, 1:20 000; Wako Chemicals Inc.) and rated as present or absent. 27 The severity of arteriolosclerosis was assessed semi-quantitatively from anterior basal ganglia and rated as none-to-mild versus moderate-to-severe. 28 The severity of atherosclerosis was evaluated from the circle of Willis, including the basilar, vertebral, anterior, middle and posterior cerebral arteries and their proximal branches, and classified as none-to-mild versus moderate-to-severe for the analysis. 29 Cerebral amyloid angiopathy (CAA) was assessed from the four neocortical regions by scoring the amyloid deposition in the meningeal and parenchymal vessels. 15 Chronic cerebral infarcts, including gross and microscopic infarcts, were rated as present or absent. 18 Statistical analysis Initially, we examined the burden of grey matter ARTAG in three brain regions and examined the associations of ARTAG pathology with demographics (age at death, sex and education) using t -tests or χ 2 tests as appropriate. To compare the demographic, clinical and neuropathological characteristics in older adults with and without Alzheimer''s dementia, we also used t- tests, Wilcoxon rank sum tests or χ 2 tests as appropriate. We then used several regression models to address different associations. All models included terms to control for age at death, sex and education. First, we ran multiple logistic regression models adjusted for demographics to examine the associations of grey matter ARTAG for each region with a wide spectrum of AD (Braak stage severity, CERAD score and pathological diagnosis of AD) and other pathologies including LATE-NC, neocortical LBs, arteriolosclerosis, atherosclerosis, CAA and chronic cerebral infarcts. Second, we ran separate logistic regression models to quantify the associations of grey matter ARTAG for each region with the odds of Alzheimer''s dementia. We initially controlled for age at death, sex and education, and subsequently we adjusted for other neuropathological indices (AD, LATE-NC, LBs, HS, atherosclerosis, arteriolosclerosis, CAA and chronic cerebral infarcts). Third, we used multivariable linear mixed models to examine the associations of grey matter ARTAG for each region with the change in global cognition. Cognitive measures from multiple years were used as the longitudinal outcome, with years before death as the time scale. The interaction term of regional grey matter ARTAG and time estimates the association of region-specific grey matter ARTAG with the cognitive slope. A significant and negative coefficient for the interaction would indicate that a greater burden of region-specific grey matter ARTAG is associated with faster decline. Finally, we repeated the fully adjusted separate models that controlled for demographics and common age-related brain pathologies to examine the associations of grey matter ARTAG in each of the three regions with decline in five specific cognitive domains. All the statistical analyses were programmed in SAS/STAT software, version 9.4 (SAS Institute Inc.). A nominal threshold of P < 0.05 was used for statistical significance. Results A total of 442 participants were included in this study, of whom 374 (85%) were non-Latinos White, 56 (13%) were non-Latinos Black, 11 (2%) were Latinos, and 1 (0.2%) did not self-identify in any of these groups. Participants were extremely educated, averaging 16.5 years of education, of which 143 were male and 299 were female. Age at death ranged between 69.64 and 107.67 years. Detailed demographic, clinical and neuropathological characteristics of participants are displayed in Table 1 and Supplementary Table 2 . Table 1. Characteristics of all participants with and without Alzheimer''s dementia Characteristics All ( n = 442) Alzheimer''s dementia not present ( n = 249) Alzheimer''s dementia present ( n = 193) P -value Demographic Age (years), mean (SD) 90.2 (7) 88.54 (6.97) 92.37 (6.44) <0.001 a Male sex, n (%) 143 (32.35) 89 (35.74) 54 (27.98) 0.083 b Education (years), mean (SD) 16.57 (3.76) 16.69 (3.88) 16.41 (3.61) 0.440 a Clinical characteristics MMSE (proximate to death), mean (SD) 21.53 (9.05) 27.43 (2.65) 13.91 (8.68) <0.001 c Global cognition, lv, mean (SD) −0.91 (1.20) −0.09 (0.62) −1.98 (0.90) <0.001 a Episodic memory, lv, mean (SD) −0.81 (1.37) 0.07 (0.82) −1.95 (1.05) <0.001 a Semantic memory, lv, mean (SD) −1.14 (1.46) −0.26 (0.76) −2.27 (1.38) <0.001 a Working memory, lv, mean (SD) −0.72 (1.15) −0.07 (0.74) −1.71 (0.82) <0.001 a Perceptual speed, d lv, mean (SD) −1.01 (1.03) −0.46 (0.83) −1.71 (0.82) <0.001 a Visuospatial ability, e lv, mean (SD) −0.42 (1.01) 0.03 (0.79) −1.01 (0.95) <0.001 a Pathological characteristics Grey matter ARTAG Rating – – – – Amygdala, n (%) None 139 (31.40) 104 (41.77) 35 (18.13) <0.001 b – Mild 112 (25.30) 63 (25.30) 49 (25.39) – – Moderate–severe 191 (43.21) 82 (32.93) 109 (56.48) – Anterior temporal tip, n (%) None 192 (43.84) 139 (56.5) 53 (27.60) <0.001 b – Mild 110 (25.11) 60 (24.39) 50 (26.04) – – Moderate–severe 136 (31.05) 47 (19.11) 89 (46.35) – Superior frontal, n (%) None 303 (68.86) 205 (82.33) 98 (51.31) <0.001 b – Mild 87 (19.77) 33 (13.25) 54 (28.27) – – Moderate–severe 50 (11.36) 11 (4.42) 39 (20.42) – Pathological diagnosis of AD, n (%) 305 (69) 142 (57.03) 163 (84.46) <0.001 b AD pathology burden, mean (SD) 0.72 (0.60) 0.53 (0.47) 1.01 (0.65) <0.001 c Lewy bodies, n (%) 122 (27.60) 48 (19.2) 74 (38.34) <0.001 b LATE-NC, f stage 2/3, n (%) 158 (35.83) 58 (23.39) 100 (51.81) <0.001 b Hippocampal sclerosis, f n (%) 41 (9.30) 11(4.44) 30 (15.54) <0.001 b Cerebral infarcts, f n (%) 243 (54.98) 129 (51.81) 114 (59.07) 0.128 b Arteriolosclerosis, d n (%) 137 (31.14) 67 (26.91) 70 (36.65) 0.028 b Atherosclerosis, f n (%) 135 (30.61) 60 (24.19) 75 (38.86) <0.001 b Open in a new tab AD = Alzheimer''s disease; ARTAG = ageing-related tau astrogliopathy; LATE-NC = limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy–neuropathological change; lv = last evaluation; MMSE = Mini Mental State Examination. a t- tests. b χ 2 tests. c Wilcoxon rank sum tests. d Data missing for two participants. e Data missing for four participants. f Data missing for one participant. Burden of grey matter ARTAG Grey matter ARTAG pathology ( Fig. 1A ) was present in 324 (73%) of the 442 autopsied brains, of which 278 (86%) were non-Latinos White, 37 (11%) were non-Latinos Black, 8 (2%) were Latinos, and 1 (0.3%) did not identify as a member of any of these groups. The amygdala exhibited grey matter ARTAG pathology most frequently, with 303 participants (68%), followed by the anterior temporal tip with 246 participants (56%) and the superior frontal cortex with 137 participants (31%) ( Table 1 and Fig. 1B ). Severe grey matter ARTAG pathology was also most prevalent in the amygdala and least common in the superior frontal cortex ( Fig. 1B ). Grey matter ARTAG severity in all three brain regions was positively associated with age (all three P -values <0.001), but not with either sex or education (all three P -values >0.05). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Grey matter ageing-related tau astrogliopathy in the brain. [ A ( i – vi )] AT8 immunohistochemistry shows the severity of ARTAG pathology (none, mild or moderate–severe) in the grey matter location of the superior frontal cortex. ( B ) The bar plot shows the burden of grey matter ARTAG in three brain regions: amygdala, anterior temporal tip and superior frontal cortex. The amygdala region has a more severe level of grey matter ARTAG than both neocortical regions. ARTAG = ageing-related tau astrogliopathy. Notably, in most of the individuals, grey matter ARTAG extended to the neocortical region once already present in the amygdala ( Supplementary Fig. 2 ). More than 90% of individuals with an affected anterior temporal tip (228 of 246) and superior frontal cortex (132 of 137) exhibited ARTAG pathology in the amygdala. A small number of individuals with ARTAG in the anterior temporal pole (18 of 246) and superior frontal cortex (5 of 137) showed no grey matter ARTAG pathology in the amygdala. Furthermore, the severity of grey matter ARTAG in the anterior temporal tip and superior frontal cortex increased as the severity of grey matter ARTAG in the amygdala increased ( P < 0.001; Supplementary Fig. 2 ). We also counted TSAs and GFAs in all three brain regions. Of the 442 autopsied brains, 306 (67%) participants had TSAs and fewer participants ( n = 57, 13%) had GFAs in the amygdala. The distributions of TSAs and GFAs were similar across the neocortical regions ( Supplementary Table 3 ). McNemar''s tests between region-specific TSAs and GFAs were highly significant (amygdala, McNemar χ 2 = 267.01, P < 0.001; anterior temporal tip, McNemar χ 2 = 190.15, P < 0.001; superior frontal, McNemar χ 2 = 39.18, P < 0.001), suggesting that TSAs were more common in the grey matter location of both amygdala and neocortical regions, in comparison to GFAs. Associations of grey matter ARTAG with other brain pathologies Initially, we examined the distribution of grey matter ARTAG burdens in each region by ADNC level and other brain pathologies ( Fig. 2 and Supplementary Fig. 3 ). Figure 2 shows that in persons with no/low ADNC ( n = 137), there were 60 cases with grey matter ARTAG in the amygdala, 37 cases with grey matter ARTAG in the anterior temporal tip and 15 cases in the superior frontal cortex. This number increased markedly with intermediate ADNC and further with high ADNC. A polychoric correlation test showed that grey matter ARTAG in each region was positively associated with the level of ADNC (polychoric correlation for amygdala = 0.509, P -value <0.001; anterior temporal tip = 0.635, P -value <0.001; and superior frontal = 0.603, P -value <0.001). Supplementary Fig. 3 displays a similar pattern; the severity of grey matter ARTAG was increased with higher Braak stage and CERAD score. In logistic regression analyses after controlling demographics, we found that more severe grey matter ARTAG pathology in all three brain regions was also associated with the pathological diagnosis of AD, higher Braak stage and CERAD scores ( Table 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Distribution of grey matter ageing-related tau astrogliopathy burdens in each region by Alzheimer''s disease neuropathological change level. ( A ) Frequency of amygdala grey matter ARTAG in subjects with no/low ADNC, intermediate ADNC and high ADNC. ( B ) Frequency of anterior temporal tip grey matter ARTAG in subjects with no/low ADNC, intermediate ADNC and high ADNC. ( C ) Frequency of superior frontal grey matter ARTAG in subjects with no/low ADNC, intermediate ADNC and high ADNC. The bar diagrams in A – C show that severity of grey matter ARTAG in each region is positively correlated with the severity of ADNC. ADNC = Alzheimer''s disease neuropathological change; ARTAG = ageing-related tau astrogliopathy. Table 2. Association of grey matter ageing-related tau astrogliopathy with Alzheimer''s disease and other brain pathology Model Grey matter ARTAG, region Pathological diagnosis of AD Braak stage severity CERAD score severity LATE-NC Lewy bodies 1 Amygdala 2.49 (1.90–3.26) P < 0.001 2.67 (2.07–3.43) P < 0.001 2.54 (2.03–3.18) P < 0.001 1.58 (1.24–2.03) P < 0.001 1.08 (0.77–1.50) P = 0.646 2 Anterior temporal tip 3.04 (2.23–4.13) P < 0.001 3.93 (2.98–5.18) P < 0.001 3.19 (2.52–4.04) P < 0.001 2.00 (1.56–2.56) P < 0.001 1.42 (1.02–1.98) P = 0.037 3 Superior frontal 3.85 (2.39–6.22) P < 0.001 5.38 (3.83–7.57) P < 0.001 3.39 (2.52–4.55) P < 0.001 1.90 (1.42–2.54) P < 0.001 1.66 (1.24–2.23) P < 0.001 Open in a new tab Separate logistic regression models with five separate outcomes were used to examine the associations of region-specific grey matter ARTAG severity with AD and other brain pathologies. All models include terms to control for age at death, sex and education. Values in cells are the odds ratio (95% confidence interval), P -value. ARTAG = ageing-related tau astrogliopathy; LATE-NC = limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy–neuropathological change. We also examined the association of each region-specific grey matter ARTAG severity with other common brain pathologies. Grey matter ARTAG pathology in each of the three regions was associated with advanced LATE-NC, while the presence of LBs was associated with grey matter ARTAG pathology for only two neocortical regions (anterior temporal and superior frontal; Table 2 ). We did not observe associations of regional grey matter ARTAG with arteriolosclerosis, atherosclerosis, CAA and cerebral infarcts (all P -values >0.1, data not shown). Bonferroni correction was applied for multiple comparisons. We found that the association of region-specific grey matter ARTAG pathology with a pathological diagnosis of AD, Braak neurofibrillary tangle severity, CERAD scores and LATE-NC remained. The association between superior frontal grey matter ARTAG and LBs was also retained, but the association between anterior temporal tip grey matter ARTAG and LBs did not survive after Bonferroni correction. Next, we examined the region-specific association of grey matter ARTAG with amyloid-β and tau tangle burden. We found that superior frontal cortex grey matter ARTAG was associated with superior frontal amyloid-β [estimate = 0.37, standard error (SE) = 0.05, P < 0.001] and tau-tangle burden (estimate = 1.01, SE = 0.071, P < 0.001) after adjusting for demographics and other common brain pathologies. Similar associations were seen between amygdala grey matter ARTAG and amygdala amyloid-β (estimate = 0.24, SE = 0.04, P < 0.001) and tau-tangle burden (estimate = 0.61, SE = 0.08, P < 0.001). We were unable to examine region-specific associations for the anterior temporal tip at this time because amyloid-β and tau-tangle burden were not assessed for that region. Furthermore, we examined the association of region-specific grey matter ARTAG with the various stages of LATE-NC using polychotomous logistic regression analyses, and we found that grey matter ARTAG in all three regions was associated with LATE-NC stage 3 [superior frontal cortex, odds ratio (OR) = 2.55, 95% confidence interval (CI) = 1.80, 3.61, P < 0.001; anterior temporal tip, OR = 2.57, 95% CI = 1.90, 3.47, P < 0.001; and amygdala, OR = 2.08, 95% CI = 1.54, 2.82, P < 0.001]. In addition, amygdala grey matter ARTAG was associated with LATE-NC stage 1 (OR = 1.64, 95% CI = 1.18, 2.29, P = 0.003). Grey matter ARTAG and Alzheimer''s dementia One hundred and ninety-three (44%) of the participants were diagnosed with Alzheimer''s dementia. Initially, we examined whether grey matter ARTAG in each of the regions was associated with Alzheimer''s dementia. In comparison to individuals without grey matter ARTAG, the odds of Alzheimer''s dementia were 3.04-, 2.10- and 1.83-fold higher in those with more moderate-to-severe grey matter ARTAG pathology present in the superior frontal region, the anterior temporal tip or the amygdala, respectively ( Table 3 ). Next, to test the hypothesis that the association of grey matter ARTAG in each region with Alzheimer''s dementia was not explained by AD and other common neuropathologies, we further controlled for AD and other neurodegenerative and vascular pathologies. In the fully controlled model, only grey matter ARTAG in the superior frontal region was associated with higher odds of Alzheimer''s dementia ( Table 3 ). Alzheimer''s dementia was not associated with grey matter ARTAG at the amygdala or at the anterior temporal tip. Table 3. Association of grey matter ageing-related tau astrogliopathy with Alzheimer''s dementia Predictor Outcome: Alzheimer''s dementia Model A Model B Model C Model D Model E Model F Grey matter ARTAG, superior frontal 3.04 (2.20–4.22) P < 0.001 – – 1.71 (1.16–2.52) P = 0.006 – – Grey matter ARTAG, anterior temporal tip – 2.10 (1.65–2.68) P < 0.001 – – 1.16 (0.85–1.59) P = 0.335 – Grey matter ARTAG, amygdala – – 1.83 (1.43–2.33) P < 0.001 – – 1.18 (0.87–1.60) P = 0.264 Ad pathology score – – – 3.60 (2.19–5.92) P < 0.001 4.22 (2.59–7.05)