iopathy pathology with Alzheimer''s dementia and rate of change in global cognition in persons with no, mild and moderate-to-severe grey matter ageing-related tau astrogliopathy. ( A ) The three curved lines represent the estimated probability of dementia after adjusting for age at death, sex, education, AD pathology score, LBs, LATE-NC, HS, cerebral infarcts, arteriolosclerosis, CAA and atherosclerosis. ( B ) The three lines represent the mean cognitive trajectory for female aged 90 years with 16 years of education and mean AD pathology score of 0.72 units without other brain pathologies (LBs, LATE-NC, HS, cerebral infarcts, arteriolosclerosis, CAA and atherosclerosis). AD = Alzheimer''s disease; ARTAG = ageing-related tau astrogliopathy; CAA = cerebral amyloid angiopathy; HS = hippocampal sclerosis; LATE-NC = limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy–neuropathological change; LBs = Lewy bodies. Grey matter ARTAG and global cognitive decline Using data from cognitive assessments over multiple years before death (mean = 10.12 years, SD = 6.24 years, range 1–27 years), we examined the association of grey matter ARTAG in each region with cognitive decline. Grey matter ARTAG in each region was associated with decline in global cognition after controlling for age, sex and education. Next, we adjusted for common age-related pathologies to determine whether these relationships persisted above and beyond other pathologies. We found that the association of grey matter ARTAG pathology remained associated with cognitive decline only in the superior frontal region (estimate = −0.014, SE = 0.006, P = 0.022) while neither amygdala grey matter ARTAG nor anterior temporal tip ARTAG was associated with decline in global cognition ( Table 4 ). Figure 3B shows the estimated decline for persons with each level of grey matter ARTAG (none, mild and moderate-to-severe) in the superior frontal region. Table 4. Association of grey matter ageing-related tau astrogliopathy with the rate of decline in global cognition Predictor Outcome: cognitive decline Model A Model B Model C Model D Model E Model F Grey matter ARTAG, superior frontal −0.045 (0.006, <0.001) – – −0.013 (0.006, 0.022) – – Grey matter ARTAG, anterior temporal tip – −0.03 (0.005, <0.001) – – −0.001 (0.005, 0.982) – Grey matter ARTAG, amygdala – – −0.025 (0.005, <0.001) – – −0.001 (0.005, 0.978) AD pathology score – – – −0.063 (0.007, <0.001) −0.072 (0.007, <0.001) −0.072 (0.007, <0.001) LATE-NC – – – −0.028 (0.008, <0.001) −0.030 (0.008, <0.001) −0.030 (0.008, <0.001) Lewy bodies – – – −0.022 (0.008, 0.006) −0.022 (0.008, 0.007) −0.022 (0.008, 0.006) Hippocampal sclerosis – – – −0.044 (0.013, <0.001) −0.045 (0.013, <0.001) −0.045 (0.013, <0.001) Atherosclerosis – – – 0.002 (0.008, 0.850) −0.001 (0.008, 0.954) 0.001 (0.008, 0.882) Cerebral infarcts – – – −0.018 (0.007, 0.013) −0.018 (0.007, 0.015) −0.018 (0.007, 0.012) Arteriolosclerosis – – – −0.015 (0.008, 0.049) −0.016 (0.008, 0.042) −0.016 (0.008, 0.049) Cerebral amyloid angiopathy – – – 0.016 (0.008, 0.034) 0.014 (0.008, 0.072) 0.014 (0.008, 0.065) Open in a new tab In a series of mixed-effects models, we examined associations of regional grey matter ARTAG severity with longitudinal changes in cognitive functions. In each model, global cognition was the outcome. Models A, B and C were all adjusted for age at death, sex and education, but with different ARTAG measures. Models D, E and F were all adjusted for age at death, sex, education, AD pathology score, LATE-NC, Lewy bodies, hippocampal sclerosis, total cerebral infarcts, atherosclerosis, cerebral amyloid angiopathy and arteriolosclerosis, but again with different ARTAG measures. Values in cells are estimated coefficients (SE, P -value). AD = Alzheimer''s disease; ARTAG = ageing-related tau astrogliopathy; LATE-NC = limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy–neuropathological change; SE = standard error. To check whether the association of superior frontal grey matter ARTAG with cognitive decline differs when ADNC is present, we added a term for the three-way interaction of grey matter ARTAG from the superior frontal region, ADNC and time. This interaction was not significant ( P = 0.347), suggesting that the relationship between grey matter ARTAG and cognitive decline does not differ with the pathological diagnosis of AD. Grey matter ARTAG and decline in cognitive domains To determine whether the associations of grey matter ARTAG with cognitive decline are seen across the cognitive domains, we assessed the relationship of grey matter ARTAG in each region to change in five specific cognitive domains separately. A higher level of grey matter ARTAG pathology in the superior frontal region was associated with faster decline in episodic memory (estimate = −0.017, SE = 0.007, P = 0.012) and semantic memory (estimate = −0.018, SE = 0.007, P = 0.012), but not with the other cognitive domains ( Table 5 ). In separate analyses, amygdala grey matter ARTAG and anterior temporal tip grey matter ARTAG were not associated with decline in any of the cognitive domains ( Table 5 ). Table 5. Association of grey matter ageing-related tau astrogliopathy with the rate of decline in five cognitive domains Outcome Model Predictor (grey matter ARTAG region) Estimate (SE, P -value) Episodic memory A Superior frontal −0.017 (0.007, 0.012) B Anterior temporal tip −0.005 (0.006, 0.366) C Amygdala −0.006 (0.006, 0.285) Semantic memory A Superior frontal −0.018 (0.007, 0.012) B Anterior temporal tip 0.007 (0.006, 0.296) C Amygdala 0.002 (0.006, 0.710) Working memory A Superior frontal −0.003 (0.005, 0.512) B Anterior temporal tip 0.007 (0.004, 0.096) C Amygdala 0.008 (0.004, 0.051) Perceptual speed A Superior frontal −0.008 (0.006, 0.202) B Anterior temporal tip 0.002 (0.005, 0.652) C Amygdala 0.002 (0.005, 0.726) Visuospatial ability A Superior frontal −0.008 (0.005, 0.163) B Anterior temporal tip 0.004 (0.005, 0.380) C Amygdala 0.001 (0.004, 0.930) Open in a new tab Multiple series of mixed-effects models were used to examine associations of region-specific grey matter ARTAG severity separately with longitudinal changes in cognitive domains. All models include terms to control for age at death, sex, education, AD pathology score, LATE-NC, Lewy bodies, hippocampal sclerosis, total cerebral infarcts, atherosclerosis, cerebral amyloid angiopathy and arteriolosclerosis. Values in cells are estimated coefficients (SE, P -value). ARTAG = ageing-related tau astrogliopathy; LATE-NC = limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy–neuropathological change; SE = standard error. Additional analyses Given that grey matter ARTAG is less frequent in the superior frontal region than in the amygdala and anterior temporal tip and that most participants with superior frontal grey matter ARTAG pathology also had grey matter ARTAG in other regions, we ran sensitivity analyses to examine whether the relationship of superior frontal grey matter ARTAG with Alzheimer''s dementia and decline in global cognition and episodic and semantic memory was affected by the burden of ARTAG in other brain regions. We repeated our fully controlled models including terms for amygdala grey matter ARTAG and anterior temporal tip grey matter ARTAG; the association between superior frontal grey matter ARTAG and Alzheimer''s dementia persisted ( Supplementary Table 4 ). The association of superior frontal grey matter ARTAG with decline in global cognition and episodic and semantic memory also remained after adjusting for amygdala and anterior temporal tip grey matter ARTAG ( Supplementary Table 5 ). Discussion This clinical–pathological study of 442 older persons demonstrates that cortical grey matter ARTAG pathology is common in older individuals with and without Alzheimer''s dementia and has strong associations with AD pathology and LATE-NC and a weaker association with LB pathology. Even after controlling for these pathologies, the severity of grey matter ARTAG, particularly in the superior frontal region, has an independent association with Alzheimer''s dementia. Moreover, severe grey matter ARTAG in the superior frontal cortex was associated with faster decline in cognition, and specifically, episodic memory and semantic memory. Importantly, the association of grey matter ARTAG with cognitive decline did not differ by the presence of the pathological diagnosis of AD. These findings provide new evidence that the severity of grey matter ARTAG pathology, particularly in the superior frontal region, is deleterious to brain health and has independent contributions to late-life cognitive decline and Alzheimer''s dementia. Grey matter ARTAG is common in ageing brains, yet the frequency of grey matter ARTAG varies in studies of older persons. Higher frequencies have been seen frequently in the brains of persons who died at older age, with grey matter ARTAG found in 68% of a large population-based study of 687 individuals with a mean age of 73 years. 4 In our study of community-dwelling older adults, we also found that grey matter ARTAG is frequent, present in 73% of the group overall, and in more than three-quarters of those with Alzheimer''s dementia (86%) and nearly two-thirds of those without Alzheimer''s dementia (63%). In contrast, the 90+ study of 185 individuals showed a lower frequency (25%) of grey matter ARTAG. 6 Differences in the characteristics of participants and brain regions might account for discrepancies in the reported frequency of grey matter ARTAG pathology. Our findings are also consistent with other studies, 4-6 in that the amygdala is particularly vulnerable to ARTAG, and TSAs are typically the most frequent ARTAG pathology compared with GFAs. Our data are also consistent with spread of ARTAG from the limbic to the neocortical regions, given that we only occasionally see neocortical ARTAG without the presence of limbic ARTAG (4.7%, 21 of 442). 4 ARTAG has been described as a common pathology in the brains of those with AD pathology, with grey matter ARTAG found in 53% of individuals with pathologically confirmed AD. 4 In this study, grey matter ARTAG was strongly associated with the pathological diagnosis of AD and with both Braak stage and CERAD severity. The association of grey matter ARTAG with Braak stage severity is consistent with other studies and suggests that neuronal tau pathology and astrocytic tau pathology are related in older individuals. 6 , 30 The associations of grey matter ARTAG with neuritic plaque and LBs are interesting and require further study to replicate these findings. Furthermore, consistent with other studies, 8 , 9 grey matter ARTAG is also strongly associated with LATE-NC. Experimental evidence demonstrates that TDP-43 can enhance p-tau accumulation and tau-driven neurotoxicity in the simple animal Caenorhabditis elegans model 31 and that TDP-43 can be used as an agent to activate tau aggregation in vitro . 32 Thus, it is plausible that TDP-43 might increase the level of ARTAG or, alternatively, the factor(s) responsible for the accumulation of grey matter ARTAG might also predispose to TDP-43 mis-metabolism, alpha-synuclein aggregation or AD proteinopathy. Taken together, these associations might suggest a shared pathological cascade or, potentially, shared genetic pleiotropy between astrocytic and neuronal proteinopathies in ageing. Thus, in addition to targeting specific proteinopathies, it is likely also to be targeting overarching mechanisms of neurodegeneration for prevention and treatment of dementia. In addition to observing ARTAG pathology in AD brains, the occurrence of ARTAG has also been noted in brains impacted by traumatic brain injury and/or chronic traumatic encephalopathy. 33-36 Studies have shown a potential link between traumatic brain injury and the deposition of ARTAG pathology in older brains; however, further research is warranted to elucidate this association. 33 , 36 In contrast, recent studies have underscored a clear differentiation between cortical ARTAG and chronic traumatic encephalopathy pathology by highlighting that chronic traumatic encephalopathy manifests primarily as intraneuronal tau lesions rather than those within glial cells. 37 There are a few clinicopathological studies that have explored the association of ARTAG with Alzheimer''s dementia or cognitive impairment. One study of 185 individuals demonstrated an association of neocortical ARTAG with clinical dementia. 6 That study was notable for examining dementia in relationship with ARTAG after controlling for age, sex and AD pathology but was restricted to those >90 years of age, and models did not account for other brain pathologies, such as LATE-NC, LBs and vascular pathologies. Also, the neocortical ARTAG measurement was derived not only from grey matter locations but also from other brain locations, such as white matter, subpial and perivascular. Thus, the regional location of brain that harbours ARTAG with respect to Alzheimer''s dementia remained unclear. 6 Another small study of 83 participants also indicated a contribution of ARTAG in worse language and visuospatial performance 7 ; however, the study examined the specific role of white matter ARTAG on cognitive profiles, and grey matter ARTAG was not examined. Moreover, we are not aware of any previous studies examining the specific association of grey matter ARTAG with decline in cognition and multiple cognitive domains. The present study specifically examines the association of grey matter ARTAG with cognitive decline and Alzheimer''s dementia and expands knowledge in four important ways. First, we find that grey matter ARTAG pathology specifically in the superior frontal region is associated with Alzheimer''s dementia. The association persists after controlling for AD and other age-related pathologies, supporting the hypothesis that grey matter ARTAG pathology in this region increases the likelihood of Alzheimer''s dementia above and over the main cause of Alzheimer''s dementia. Second, this study furthers our understanding of the detrimental consequences of grey matter ARTAG pathology on cognitive outcomes and indicates that grey matter ARTAG pathology in the superior frontal region is associated with more rapid global cognitive decline. Third, we provide evidence that superior frontal grey matter ARTAG pathology burden is related specifically to a faster rate of decline in episodic memory and semantic memory. Fourth, although the effect of grey matter ARTAG on the cognitive profile is weaker than the effects of AD and other neurodegenerative pathologies, such as LATE-NC and LBs, grey matter ARTAG might help to explain some of the unexplained variance in the late-life cognitive impairment not accounted for by other brain pathologies. 15 The mechanism by which grey matter ARTAG pathology is related to more severe cognitive impairment and decline is unknown, but several in vivo and animal studies suggest that accumulation of hyperphosphorylated tau pathology in astrocytes might increase neurotoxicity and neuronal death through blood–brain barrier dysfunction, synapse loss, water–ion imbalances or neuroinflammation. 38-43 These mechanisms were supported by a post-mortem study that showed elevated levels of connexin-43 and aquaporin-4 in brains with grey matter ARTAG. 44 Connexin-43 and aquaporin-4 are essential for the regulation of blood–brain barrier integrity, synaptic function and the glymphatic pathway, 44-47 hence dysregulation of connexin-43 and aquaporin-4 expression in individuals with grey matter ARTAG suggests that ARTAG might be an indicator of neuronal death. 44 More investigation is needed to understand the relationship between astrocyte tauopathy and neurodegeneration. This study has several strengths. Given that this study used clinical–pathological data from large community-based cohorts of older people, the results are more generalizable to the general population of older people than a clinically based group. Annual clinical follow-up rates for cognition and autopsy rates were high, reducing the risk of selection bias. Systematic clinical and neuropathological evaluations were performed blinded to each other. We developed a semiquantitative assessment method to examine the ARTAG burden that provides more granular information about ARTAG pathology. Despite the strengths, this study also has some limitations. Our study included participants with relatively high education, and the majority were older White adults. Future studies are required to extend our findings to more diverse groups with a wider range of educational attainment. This study included relatively healthy participants who agreed to autopsy, potentially limiting generalizability. Although we assessed multiple cognitive domains in relationship to grey matter ARTAG in this study, we could not assess behavioural symptoms that are commonly seen in frontotemporal dementia patients, which would provide important information. For the analytical analysis, pathological ARTAG was categorized into three levels to facilitate comparisons with brain pathologies; this characterization was data driven, but applying separate thresholds for the severity of pathology could affect our results. This study did not evaluate ARTAG pathology from subpial, subependymal or other sites. Future studies examining ARTAG from other locations will be important to understand the effects of other ARTAG subtypes with vascular and other brain pathologies and cognitive function. We did not explore the association between grey matter ARTAG and different stages of Lewy body disease pathology owing to the limited number of individuals exhibiting nigra-type Lewy body disease and amygdala-predominant Lewy body disease pathology within our participants. Supplementary Material awae250_Supplementary_Data awae250_supplementary_data.pdf (711.3KB, pdf) Acknowledgements We would like to acknowledge our research participa